var e={"tech:sketchpad":{intro:`Sketchpad is a drawing program in which a person draws straight onto a computer's display with a light pen, a pen-shaped wand that senses the glowing dot beneath its tip. Every line, arc and shape is stored as an object the machine understands, so it can be copied, moved, resized and tied to other shapes by rules.`,how:`The light pen tells the computer where on the screen it is pointing, while push buttons choose the action: draw a line, close a shape, delete, zoom. The user can set constraints, such as making two lines parallel or equal in length, and the program adjusts the drawing until every rule holds. A master drawing can be placed many times as instances; change the master and every copy changes with it.`,history:`Ivan Sutherland wrote Sketchpad at MIT for his PhD, which he completed in January 1963 with Claude Shannon as his supervisor. It ran on the TX-2, built at MIT's Lincoln Laboratory in 1958 and one of the very few machines of its day with a large display and a light pen. Sutherland presented it to the profession at the Spring Joint Computer Conference in 1963. In 1964 he became head of ARPA's Information Processing Techniques Office, where he funded graphics and interactive computing at universities. In 1968 he co-founded Evans & Sutherland with David Evans to build graphics systems for flight simulators and design. In 1988 Sketchpad earned him the Turing Award, computing's highest honour.`,facts:[`The TX-2 that ran Sketchpad had 64K words of memory (1963).`,`Sutherland was 24 when he submitted the Sketchpad thesis (1963).`,`His Turing Award came 25 years after Sketchpad (1988).`]},"tech:mouse":{intro:`NLS, the oN-Line System, is a computer workspace built for thinking and working together. A hand-held box on two wheels, called a mouse, steers a pointer across the screen; text can carry links that jump to other documents; and two people in different places can work on the same file while they see and hear each other.`,how:`As the mouse slides across a desk, two metal wheels set at right angles turn and report movement up and down and left and right, and its buttons select. The other hand plays a five-key chord keyset to give commands, so the keyboard is needed only for longer typing. Documents are stored as outlines of numbered statements, and any statement can link to any other, the idea called hypertext.`,history:`Douglas Engelbart set out his plan in October 1962 in a report called 'Augmenting Human Intellect', written at the Stanford Research Institute (SRI) in California. In 1964 his engineer Bill English built the first mouse from Engelbart's sketch, in a carved wooden case. In 1967 English and Engelbart published tests showing that the mouse beat the light pen and the joystick for picking out words on a screen. On 9 December 1968 Engelbart showed the whole system for 90 minutes to about 1,000 people in San Francisco, linked to the computer at SRI about 30 miles away. In October 1969 SRI's computer became the second node of the ARPANET. In 1970 SRI was granted the patent for an 'X-Y position indicator for a display system', the mouse.`,facts:[`The first mouse of 1964 had a single button; the 1968 demo mice had three.`,`The five-key chord keyset gave 31 different combinations (1968).`,`About 1,000 people watched the 90-minute demonstration (1968).`]},"tech:xwindow":{intro:`X is a window system for Unix workstations that works across a network. The program that owns the screen, keyboard and mouse is called the server; applications are its clients, and they can run on the same machine or on any computer of the network, opening their windows on your display as if they were local.`,how:`Clients and the server speak the X protocol: small messages such as 'draw this line in this window' go one way, and key presses and mouse clicks come back. X deliberately leaves out any fixed look, a rule its designers called mechanism, not policy. A separate program, the window manager, decides how frames and title bars behave, and toolkits on top supply buttons and menus, so different desktops can share one server.`,history:`Bob Scheifler and Jim Gettys started X at MIT in June 1984, building on W, a window system from Stanford's V operating system, which is why the new one took the next letter. It served Project Athena, MIT's campus computing programme launched in 1983 with money from DEC and IBM, which needed one display system for machines from many makers. Version 10 spread to several vendors in 1985 and 1986, and X11 arrived in September 1987 with a protocol designed to be extended without breaking old programs. In January 1988 MIT set up the X Consortium so that vendors could steer it together. In 1988 and 1989 rival toolkits, OPEN LOOK from Sun and AT&T and Motif from the Open Software Foundation, fought over how X desktops should look. In 1992 XFree86 brought X to ordinary PCs running free Unix-like systems.`,facts:[`X11 Release 1 shipped on 15 September 1987.`,`Project Athena, which paid for X, ran from 1983 to 1991.`,`The name X is simply the letter after W, its predecessor (1984).`]},"tech:hig":{intro:`Human interface guidelines are a platform maker's rulebook for how programs should look and behave: where the menus go, what each standard command is called, and how dialogs, buttons and warnings work. When every program follows the same rules, skills learned in one carry over to the next, and the whole platform feels like one machine.`,how:`The book sets out principles first, then detail. Apple's version names ideas such as direct manipulation, see-and-point instead of remember-and-type, consistency, user control, feedback and forgiveness, meaning that actions can be undone. Then it fixes the specifics: a File menu with Open, Save and Quit, an Edit menu with Undo, Cut, Copy and Paste on the same keys in every program, and alert boxes worded and laid out the same way.`,history:`When the Macintosh launched in January 1984, its programming manual, Inside Macintosh, included a chapter of user interface guidelines, and every Mac program shared one menu bar and the same editing keys. In 1987 Addison-Wesley published 'Human Interface Guidelines: The Apple Desktop Interface' as a book anyone could buy. The same year, 1987, IBM announced Common User Access as part of its Systems Application Architecture, fixing menus, function keys and dialog layouts across its PCs, terminals and OS/2. In 1995 Microsoft published 'The Windows Interface Guidelines for Software Design' to go with Windows 95. In 2014 Google's Material Design carried the idea to phones and the web, with rules for motion, shadows and colour.`,facts:[`Mac programs shared Command-Z, X, C and V for Undo, Cut, Copy and Paste from 1984.`,`IBM's Common User Access made F1 the help key across its systems (1987).`]},"tech:usability":{intro:`Usability testing means watching real people try to use a product and treating every stumble as a fault in the design, not in the person. Instead of asking users what they think of a program, testers give them real tasks, then note how long they take, where they hesitate and what goes wrong.`,how:`A tester gives a participant a task, such as 'print page three', and asks them to think aloud while working, saying what they expect and what puzzles them. Observers stay silent, often behind a one-way mirror with a video camera running. A cheaper cousin, heuristic evaluation, has a few reviewers check the screens against a short list of rules, such as 'show the system's status' and 'speak the user's language'. Small rounds of testing, repeated after each fix, find more problems than one big study.`,history:`Xerox tested the design of its Star office workstation on users throughout its development, before its launch in 1981. In 1982 Clayton Lewis at IBM described the thinking-aloud method for studying how people use computers. In 1988 Don Norman's book 'The Psychology of Everyday Things' argued that when people fail with a device, the design is usually to blame. In 1990 Jakob Nielsen and Rolf Molich presented heuristic evaluation, a quick way for a few reviewers to find most usability problems. In 1993 Nielsen's book 'Usability Engineering' gave companies a full method, and Nielsen and Tom Landauer showed with a mathematical model that a handful of testers finds most of the problems. In 1998 Nielsen and Norman founded the Nielsen Norman Group consultancy.`,facts:[`By Nielsen and Landauer's model, five test users uncover about 85% of a design's problems (1993).`,`Heuristic evaluation began with nine rules (1990); Nielsen's revised list had ten (1994).`]},"tech:fourgl":{intro:`Fourth-generation languages, or 4GLs, let people describe the result they want, such as a report, a query or a data-entry screen, instead of writing every step of the program. Where third-generation languages like COBOL need a programmer to spell out loops and files, a 4GL fills in the routine work itself, so business analysts can build their own applications.`,how:`A 4GL sits on top of a database. A statement like 'TABLE FILE SALES SUM AMOUNT BY REGION' names the data, the totals and the grouping, and the tool works out how to read the records, sort them and lay out the printed page. Screen painters let users draw a form and attach it to database fields. The price is control: unusual logic is awkward, the generated programs can be slow, and the code runs only on that vendor's product.`,history:`In 1975 Information Builders of New York released FOCUS, a report language for IBM mainframes. In 1979 Software AG of Germany launched Natural, a language for its Adabas database. In 1980 Ashton-Tate's dBase II brought a similar command language to microcomputers. In 1982 the consultant James Martin made the term famous with his book 'Application Development Without Programmers', arguing that users would soon build most business systems themselves. In July 1991 Powersoft's PowerBuilder let teams paint Windows screens over SQL databases, and it became a favourite of corporate developers. In 1995 Sybase bought Powersoft in a share deal worth more than $900 million.`,facts:[`Sybase paid more than $900 million in shares for PowerBuilder's maker (1995).`,`James Martin predicted in 1982 that end users, not programmers, would build most business applications.`]},"tech:imageedit":{intro:`Digital image editing turns a personal computer into a photographer's darkroom and a painter's studio. A scanned photograph is a grid of coloured dots, and the editor can brighten, sharpen, recolour or retouch any part of it, paint new strokes, or apply filters that blur or distort, all without chemicals and with the last mistake easily undone.`,how:`The program holds the image as numbers for the red, green and blue of every pixel. Tools change those numbers: a brush paints them, a marquee or lasso fences off an area so that changes stay inside it, and levels and curves remap brightness across the whole picture. Filters run the same small calculation on every pixel and its neighbours, which is why image editing needs a fast processor and plenty of memory.`,history:`In 1987 Thomas Knoll, a PhD student at the University of Michigan, wrote a program called Display to show greyscale pictures on his black-and-white Macintosh Plus. His brother John, who worked at the film effects studio Industrial Light & Magic, saw it could become a full image editor, and in 1988, after about 200 copies had shipped with Barneyscan slide scanners, the brothers licensed it to Adobe. Adobe released Photoshop 1.0 for the Macintosh on 19 February 1990. In 1991 version 2.0 added paths and the CMYK colours used by printing presses. In 1994 version 3.0 introduced layers, so each part of a picture could be kept and edited separately.`,facts:[`Photoshop 1.0 cost $895 and ran only on the Macintosh (1990).`,`A 1,000 by 1,000 pixel colour photo needs 3MB of memory, far more than most PCs had (1990).`,`Before Adobe, the program had been called Display and then ImagePro (1988).`]},"tech:compdocs":{intro:`Compound documents let one file hold pieces made by different programs: a letter from the word processor with a live chart from the spreadsheet inside it. The pieces stay connected to the program that made them, so changing the numbers in the spreadsheet updates the chart in the letter, and the user thinks about documents rather than applications.`,how:`Dynamic Data Exchange (DDE) is a conversation between two running programs: one asks for a named item, such as a range of cells, and the other sends it, and keeps sending it whenever it changes. Object Linking and Embedding (OLE) goes further. A linked object points to a file stored elsewhere; an embedded object keeps its data inside the document. Double-clicking either one starts the program that owns it, so the user can edit it.`,history:`The Xerox Star office workstation of 1981 already let text, pictures and charts sit together in one document. Microsoft built DDE into Windows 2.0 in December 1987, and spreadsheets used it to feed live figures to other programs. In 1990 Microsoft introduced OLE 1.0 for linking and embedding objects, and in 1991 Apple's System 7 answered with Publish and Subscribe for sharing live sections between documents. In 1993 OLE 2 let an embedded object be edited in place, with the spreadsheet's menus and toolbars appearing inside the word processor's window. In 1993 Apple, IBM and Novell began OpenDoc as an open alternative built from small parts, but Apple cancelled it in March 1997.`,facts:[`Excel 5 and Word 6 were among the first programs with in-place OLE 2 editing (1993).`,`OpenDoc lasted about four years before Apple dropped it (1997).`]},"tech:appframework":{intro:`A GUI application framework is a ready-made, empty application. It already opens windows, shows menus, handles the mouse and keyboard, prints and saves files; the programmer adds only the parts that make it a drawing program or a spreadsheet, by writing subclasses that change what the standard objects do.`,how:`In a plain graphical system every program must run its own event loop, checking for each click and key press and redrawing its windows itself. A framework turns this round: it runs the loop and calls the programmer's code when something happens, a pattern nicknamed 'don't call us, we'll call you'. Standard classes for documents, views and commands give undo, scrolling and printing for free. Visual tools such as Interface Builder let the programmer draw windows and connect buttons to code without writing layout by hand.`,history:`In 1979 Trygve Reenskaug, visiting Xerox PARC, described model-view-controller, a way to keep a Smalltalk program's data apart from the windows that show it. In 1985 Apple released MacApp, written in Object Pascal, because programming the Macintosh directly was so hard. On 18 September 1989 NeXT shipped NeXTSTEP 1.0, whose Application Kit in Objective-C and Interface Builder, by Jean-Marie Hullot, made building a full application remarkably fast. In 1990 Tim Berners-Lee used those tools at CERN to write the first web browser in a few months. In 1991 Borland's ObjectWindows Library brought a framework to Windows, and in 1992 Microsoft's Foundation Classes followed with Microsoft C/C++ 7.0.`,facts:[`The NeXT Computer that carried NeXTSTEP cost $6,500 (1988).`,`Berners-Lee's browser-editor, WorldWideWeb, was built on NeXTSTEP in under three months (1990).`]},"tech:controls":{intro:`Custom controls are ready-made screen parts sold by one company for use in another company's programs: a data grid, a chart, a calendar, a fax sender. A developer drops one onto a form in a visual builder, sets its properties, and gains in minutes a feature that would have taken weeks to write.`,how:`A control is a small library that follows a fixed contract with its host. It tells the visual builder which properties it has, such as colour or font, which events it fires, such as Click, and which methods can be called on it. The builder lists these in its property window, so the bought control behaves just like the built-in buttons and text boxes. Vendors sell controls in thick catalogues and by mail order, and some programmers build entire applications from them.`,history:`In 1988 Alan Cooper sold Microsoft a visual form designer called Ruby, designed so that new controls could be added to it. Microsoft joined it to a BASIC language and released Visual Basic 1.0 on 20 May 1991, with VBX files, Visual Basic eXtensions, for controls from other companies. By the mid-1990s more than a hundred vendors sold controls, from spreadsheet-style grids to barcode readers. In 1994 the 32-bit OCX, or OLE custom control, replaced the 16-bit VBX and worked in other tools too. In 1995 Borland's Delphi arrived with its own component library, and in 1996 Microsoft renamed its controls ActiveX controls.`,facts:[`Visual Basic 1.0 launched on 20 May 1991.`,`Buyers spent about $240 million on ActiveX components in 1996, by Giga Information Group's count.`]},"tech:distobjects":{intro:`Distributed objects let a program use an object that lives on another computer as if it were in its own memory. A call such as account.withdraw(100) travels across the network, runs on the far machine and returns its answer, whatever language each side is written in and whatever hardware it runs on.`,how:`Each object's abilities are written in an Interface Definition Language (IDL), which belongs to no programming language. A compiler turns the IDL into a stub for the caller and a skeleton for the object. The stub packs the call and its arguments into a message, an Object Request Broker (ORB) finds the object and delivers it, and the skeleton unpacks it and runs the real code. The broker also offers services such as naming, so programs can find objects by name.`,history:`The Object Management Group (OMG) was founded in April 1989 by eleven companies, among them Hewlett-Packard, Sun, Data General and American Airlines, to agree on standards for objects. In October 1991 it published CORBA 1.0, the Common Object Request Broker Architecture. Brokers from different vendors could not talk to each other until CORBA 2.0 defined the Internet Inter-ORB Protocol (IIOP) in 1996. In 1996 Microsoft answered with DCOM, which stretched its COM components across a network in Windows NT 4.0. In 1997 Java's Remote Method Invocation arrived with JDK 1.1, letting Java programs call each other's objects with no IDL at all.`,facts:[`The OMG started with 11 member companies (1989).`,`It took five years, from CORBA 1.0 (1991) to IIOP (1996), for rival brokers to interoperate.`]},"tech:com":{intro:`The Component Object Model (COM) is Microsoft's standard for software parts that plug into each other. A component written in C++ can be used from Visual Basic or another language, by a program from a different company, because both sides agree only on interfaces: fixed lists of functions with a fixed layout in memory.`,how:`Every interface and every component class has a 128-bit globally unique identifier, so no two can clash. Every component supports one basic interface, IUnknown, with three functions: QueryInterface asks whether it also offers another interface, and AddRef and Release count its users so it can delete itself when the count reaches zero. The Windows registry maps identifiers to the files holding the code. Once published, an interface never changes; new abilities come as new interfaces.`,history:`COM shipped in 1993 as the foundation of OLE 2, Microsoft's system for embedding documents inside each other. In 1994 OLE custom controls, or OCX files, used COM to replace Visual Basic's older VBX add-ons. In 1996 Microsoft renamed its internet components ActiveX, so that controls could be downloaded into Internet Explorer web pages, and added Authenticode signatures to show who had made them. In 1996 Fred McLain published Exploder, a signed ActiveX control that shut down any Windows 95 computer that ran it, to prove that a signature shows who wrote code, not that it is safe. In 1996 DCOM carried COM calls across networks, and in February 2000 Windows 2000 merged it with Microsoft Transaction Server into COM+.`,facts:[`IUnknown, the interface every COM object supports, has exactly three functions (1993).`,`A COM identifier has 128 bits, about 3.4 × 10^38 possible values (1993).`]},"tech:crossui":{intro:`A cross-platform GUI toolkit gives programmers one set of windows, buttons, menus and dialogs that works the same on Windows, the Macintosh and Unix. Write the program once against the toolkit, recompile it on each system, and it runs everywhere, instead of being rewritten for each operating system's own interface library.`,how:`Toolkits take one of two routes. Some wrap each system's own controls, so a button really is a Windows button on Windows and a Motif button on Unix; programs look native, but only features every system shares are available. Others draw every control themselves on a blank window, imitating each platform's look; they behave the same everywhere and can offer more, but the imitation is never quite perfect. Either way the toolkit also hides differences in files, fonts and events.`,history:`In 1992 Julian Smart began wxWindows at the University of Edinburgh, wrapping each system's native controls. Haavard Nord and Eirik Chambe-Eng designed Qt in Norway from 1991 and founded their own company in Oslo in 1994 to sell it. On 20 May 1995 Trolltech released Qt 0.90, a C++ toolkit that drew its own widgets. In 1996 Sun's Java arrived with the AWT toolkit and the slogan 'write once, run anywhere'. In 1996 Matthias Ettrich started the KDE desktop on Qt, and worries about Qt's licence led others to start GNOME on the GTK+ toolkit in 1997. In 1998 Java's Swing, which painted every control itself, arrived with JDK 1.2.`,facts:[`Qt 0.90 was announced on Usenet on 20 May 1995.`,`Nokia bought Trolltech for well over $100 million (2008).`]},"tech:webdesign":{intro:`Visual web design tools let people build web pages the way they lay out a newsletter in a word processor: type text, drag in pictures, draw tables and links, and see the result at once. The tool writes the HTML code behind the scenes, so a shop or a club can make its own website without learning markup.`,how:`The editor shows the page roughly as a browser would draw it, a style called WYSIWYG, for 'what you see is what you get'. Each change on screen becomes tags in the file. A site view shows every page and link, warns about broken links and uploads changed files to the web server. The catch is that browsers draw HTML differently, and tools that write messy code can make pages that look right in the editor but wrong elsewhere.`,history:`In 1995 Vermeer Technologies of Cambridge, Massachusetts released FrontPage 1.0, a visual editor with site management for Windows. In January 1996 Microsoft bought Vermeer, and by 2000 FrontPage shipped with some editions of Microsoft Office. In 1996 Netscape added a page editor to Navigator Gold and Adobe launched PageMill. In December 1997 Macromedia released Dreamweaver, which professionals liked because it left their hand-written HTML untouched. In 2005 Adobe bought Macromedia, maker of Dreamweaver and Flash, for about $3.4 billion. In 2006 Microsoft discontinued FrontPage in favour of newer tools.`,facts:[`Microsoft paid about $130 million in shares for FrontPage's maker (1996).`,`Adobe paid about $3.4 billion for Macromedia (2005).`]},"tech:pkgarchive":{intro:`A package archive is one large, organised library on the internet where anyone can upload a reusable module of code and anyone can download it. Copies are kept on mirror servers around the world, every module follows a standard layout with documentation, and a programmer can search for a ready solution before writing one from scratch.`,how:`Authors upload a module to a central server, which indexes it by name and version. Mirror sites around the world copy the whole archive regularly, so downloads are fast and the archive survives if one site fails. A client program, such as CPAN.pm for Perl, fetches a module by name, unpacks it, runs its tests and installs it, and can fetch the other modules it needs.`,history:`TeX users in Britain, Germany and the United States set up the Comprehensive TeX Archive Network (CTAN) in 1992 and announced it in 1993, with matching mirror sites for typesetting macros and fonts. Perl programmers copied the idea and the name, and the Comprehensive Perl Archive Network (CPAN), organised by Jarkko Hietaniemi and Andreas König, went online in October 1995. In 2003 Python gained its own Package Index, PyPI. In 2004 Ruby got RubyGems, and in 2010 npm began doing the same for JavaScript on Node.js. By 2026 CPAN held about 45,500 distributions from about 14,500 authors.`,facts:[`CPAN went online on 26 October 1995.`,`CTAN's first three hubs were in England, Germany and the US (1992).`]},"tech:css":{intro:`Cascading Style Sheets (CSS) is a language for describing how web pages look, kept separate from the words and structure in the HTML. One short file can say that all headings are dark blue and large, that paragraphs have wide margins and that links are underlined, and every page of a site follows it.`,how:`A style sheet is a list of rules. Each rule has a selector, such as 'h1' or a class name, and declarations, such as 'color: navy'. Rules can come from the site's author, from the reader's own settings and from the browser's defaults, and the cascade decides which wins when they conflict, by importance, by how specific the selector is and by order. Because styles live in one file, changing a whole site's look means editing one place, and pages no longer need font tags and layout tricks.`,history:`Håkon Wium Lie, working with Tim Berners-Lee at CERN, proposed 'Cascading HTML Style Sheets' on 10 October 1994, and Bert Bos soon joined him. In August 1996 Microsoft's Internet Explorer 3 became the first major browser to support part of it. On 17 December 1996 the World Wide Web Consortium (W3C) published CSS level 1 as a recommendation. In May 1998 CSS2 added positioning, so designers could place boxes exactly on the page. In 2003 Dave Shea's CSS Zen Garden showed one HTML page in dozens of completely different designs, changing only the style sheet. In 2017 the Grid layout system reached every major browser.`,facts:[`Lie's first proposal came on 10 October 1994, two years before CSS1 was finished (1996).`,`Grid layout worked in all major browsers by October 2017.`]},"tech:plugins":{intro:`A browser plug-in is a separate program that runs inside a web page to show what the browser cannot handle by itself: animation, sound, video, 3D and interactive games. The page marks out a rectangle, and the plug-in, installed once on the user's computer, takes charge of everything drawn inside it.`,how:`The page names a media type, and the browser hands that region and the file's data to whichever installed plug-in claims the type, through an interface such as Netscape's plug-in API. The plug-in runs as native code with the same rights as the browser, so it is fast enough for smooth animation. Flash files stay small because they store shapes and motion rather than frames of pixels, which suits slow modems. The same power means every bug in a plug-in is a possible way into the user's computer.`,history:`Netscape Navigator 2.0 introduced plug-ins in 1996, and early ones played RealAudio sound, QuickTime films and Macromedia's Shockwave content. In 1996 Jonathan Gay's FutureWave Software launched FutureSplash Animator, and Microsoft's MSN and Disney used it on their websites. In December 1996 Macromedia bought FutureWave and renamed the product Flash. In 2002 Flash Player 6 added video, and from 2005 YouTube used Flash to play every clip. In April 2010 Steve Jobs's open letter 'Thoughts on Flash' explained why Apple's iPhone and iPad did not run it. In 2017 Adobe announced the end of Flash, and support stopped on 31 December 2020.`,facts:[`Adobe claimed Flash Player was on over 95% of internet-connected PCs (2009).`,`Flash was switched off on 31 December 2020, 24 years after its launch.`]},"tech:dhtml":{intro:`Dynamic HTML is the name for web pages that change themselves while you look at them. A script running in the browser can reach every heading, picture and paragraph on the page as an object, then move it, hide it, restyle it or rewrite its text, without asking the server for a new page.`,how:`The Document Object Model (DOM) presents the page as a tree: the document contains a body, the body contains headings and paragraphs, and each is an object with properties and events. A JavaScript function can respond to the mouse passing over a menu by changing an element's style, so a list drops down. The trouble is that each browser maker invented its own model, so scripts must test which browser they are in and do every job twice.`,history:`In June 1997 Netscape Communicator 4 offered layers, blocks of a page that scripts could move, show or hide. In October 1997 Microsoft's Internet Explorer 4 went further, making every element of the page scriptable, but in a different way from Netscape. In October 1998 the W3C published DOM Level 1, a single standard model for all browsers. In March 1999 Internet Explorer 5 added XMLHTTP, which let a script fetch fresh data from the server in the background. In 2004 Gmail, and in February 2005 Google Maps, showed how far this could go, and that same month, February 2005, Jesse James Garrett named the approach Ajax.`,facts:[`Windows 98 built Internet Explorer 4 into the operating system itself (1998).`,`Internet Explorer passed 90% of browser use, making its model the de facto standard (2002).`]},"tech:appserver":{intro:`An application server is a large program that hosts a company's business logic and handles the hard parts of serving many users at once. Programmers write components, such as 'place order' or 'transfer money', and the server supplies threads, database connections, transactions, security and recovery from crashes, so the components themselves stay simple.`,how:`Components are deployed into a container, with a descriptor file declaring how each should behave: which methods run inside a transaction, which user roles may call them. The container creates and pools component instances, opens database connections in advance and shares them, and makes sure that a transfer either completes in every database or is rolled back everywhere. Several servers can run as a cluster, so if one fails another takes over its users.`,history:`Transaction monitors such as IBM's CICS, sold from 1969, had long done this job for mainframe terminals. From 1996 Microsoft Transaction Server brought the idea to Windows components. In 1998 Sun published the Enterprise JavaBeans (EJB) 1.0 specification, IBM launched WebSphere and BEA Systems bought WebLogic. In December 1999 Sun bundled EJB and its other server standards into Java 2 Enterprise Edition (J2EE). In 2001 WebLogic and WebSphere each held about a third of the Java application server market. In 2004 the lighter Spring Framework reached version 1.0, offering many of the same services with plain Java objects, and in 2008 Oracle bought BEA for about $8.5 billion.`,facts:[`Oracle paid about $8.5 billion for BEA Systems (2008).`,`Red Hat bought the free JBoss application server for $350 million (2006).`]},"tech:depresolve":{intro:`A dependency manager installs software together with everything it needs. Ask for one program and the tool reads the list of libraries it depends on, and the libraries those depend on, picks versions that fit together, downloads them all from an online repository and installs them in the right order.`,how:`Each package carries a short description: its name, its version and the packages it needs, often with limits such as 'version 2.0 or newer'. The manager builds a graph of all these needs, checks for conflicts and works out a set of versions that satisfies every rule, which in hard cases is a genuine puzzle for the computer. Repositories publish an index of every package, so upgrading a whole system becomes a single command.`,history:`Debian's dpkg tool, from 1994, could install a package but left the user to chase its dependencies by hand. In 1998 Debian developers wrote APT, the Advanced Package Tool, and in March 1999 Debian 2.1 introduced it. In 2002 work began on Apache Maven, which fetched the Java libraries a project declared, and the libraries they needed, from a central repository; version 1.0 followed in July 2004. In March 2004 RubyGems gave Ruby the same service, and in 2010 npm did it for JavaScript. In March 2016 a programmer removed his tiny 'left-pad' package from npm, and thousands of projects that depended on it through other packages suddenly failed to build.`,facts:[`Debian 2.1, the first release with APT, had about 2,250 packages from over 400 developers (1999).`,`The left-pad package that broke thousands of builds was 11 lines long (2016).`]},"tech:wcag":{intro:`The Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) are rules for making websites usable by people with disabilities: blind people whose screen readers speak the page aloud, deaf people who need captions, people who cannot use a mouse, and people with poor eyesight or colour blindness. A site that follows them works for more people, and usually works better for everyone.`,how:`The guidelines ask for a text equivalent for every image and sound, so a screen reader has something to say; for meaning that does not depend on colour alone; for pages that can be used entirely from the keyboard; for headings and tables marked up so they make sense when read in order; and for pages that still work when style sheets or scripts are switched off. Each checkpoint has a priority, and sites claim conformance at level A, Double-A or Triple-A.`,history:`In 1990 the Americans with Disabilities Act banned discrimination against disabled people across American public life. In April 1997 the W3C launched its Web Accessibility Initiative, with support from the US government and industry. On 5 May 1999 the W3C published WCAG 1.0, with 14 guidelines and 65 checkpoints. In 2000 a blind man, Bruce Maguire, won a complaint against the Sydney Olympics organisers because their website could not be used with his screen reader. In December 2008 WCAG 2.0 regrouped the rules under four principles: perceivable, operable, understandable and robust. In 2022 the World Health Organization estimated that about 16% of the world's people live with a significant disability.`,facts:[`WCAG 1.0 had 14 guidelines and 65 checkpoints (1999).`,`Target settled a blind shoppers' lawsuit over its website for $6 million (2008).`,`About 1.3 billion people live with a significant disability (WHO, 2022).`]},"tech:abtest":{intro:`An online experiment, or A/B test, settles design arguments with evidence. Visitors are split at random: some see the current version, A, and others see a change, B, such as a new button or headline. Because the groups are otherwise alike, any difference in clicks, sign-ups or sales must be caused by the change.`,how:`Each visitor is assigned to a group by a random number tied to their cookie or account, so they keep seeing the same version. The site logs what each group does, and statistics tell whether the gap between them is bigger than chance would produce; small effects need many thousands of visitors. Teams choose their target measure in advance and watch others, such as page speed, to catch harm. Running hundreds of tests a year turns product design into a steady series of small, measured improvements.`,history:`The method comes from farm science: in 1935 the statistician Ronald Fisher, who had worked at the Rothamsted research station in England, set out randomised experiments in his book 'The Design of Experiments'. In 2000 Google ran its first A/B test, on how many results to show on each search page. In 2008 Barack Obama's presidential campaign tested versions of its sign-up page and found one that gathered about 40% more email addresses. In 2009 the designer Douglas Bowman left Google, complaining that it had tested 41 shades of blue to settle one colour. In 2012 a small change to how Microsoft's Bing showed advertisement headlines, found by experiment, became the most valuable idea in Bing's history.`,facts:[`Google ran more than 7,000 A/B tests in 2011.`,`Bing's 2012 headline test raised revenue by 12%, over $100 million a year.`,`At Microsoft only about a third of tested ideas improved the measure they aimed at (2009).`]},"tech:webservices":{intro:`Web services let programs at different companies call each other across the internet using XML, a text format both sides can read. A shop's system can ask a supplier's system for stock levels, or a travel site can book a seat with an airline, sending structured messages over ordinary web connections that pass through company firewalls.`,how:`A SOAP message is an XML envelope with an optional header and a body that names the operation and its parameters, usually sent over HTTP. A WSDL file is the contract: it lists the operations a service offers and the exact shape of every message, so tools can generate the calling code automatically. A directory standard, UDDI, is meant to let programs find services by themselves. Further standards add signatures, encryption and reliable delivery, at the cost of a thick stack of specifications.`,history:`In February 1998 the W3C published XML 1.0, and that year Dave Winer's UserLand Software, working with Microsoft, created XML-RPC for calling procedures over the web. In September 1999 the design grew into SOAP, which was sent to the Internet Engineering Task Force, and in May 2000 IBM and Microsoft backed SOAP 1.1 as a W3C note. In June 2000 Bill Gates made 'XML web services' the centre of Microsoft's new .NET strategy. In September 2000 IBM, Microsoft and Ariba announced UDDI, and in March 2001 WSDL 1.1 described how to write service contracts. In 2002 Amazon opened its catalogue to programs in both SOAP and a simpler plain-web style. In January 2006 IBM, Microsoft and SAP shut the public UDDI registry, which had found few users.`,facts:[`XML 1.0 became a W3C recommendation on 10 February 1998.`,`SOAP 1.2 became a full W3C recommendation in June 2003.`]},"tech:dotnet":{intro:`C# and the .NET Framework are Microsoft's managed programming platform. Programs are compiled not to processor instructions but to an intermediate language that a runtime turns into machine code as they run, checking types and freeing unused memory automatically. Several languages, including C# and Visual Basic, share the same runtime and class library, so their code mixes freely.`,how:`The Common Language Runtime (CLR) loads intermediate-language code and compiles each method to native code the first time it is called, which is known as just-in-time compilation. It manages memory with a garbage collector, enforces type safety and checks security permissions. A large class library covers files, networking, XML, databases, Windows Forms desktop screens and ASP.NET web pages. C# itself looks like Java and C++, but adds properties, events and delegates, which are references to methods.`,history:`In 1996 Microsoft hired Anders Hejlsberg, creator of Turbo Pascal and Delphi, away from Borland, and in 1997 Sun sued Microsoft over its changes to Java. In July 2000 Microsoft unveiled C# and the framework at its Professional Developers Conference in Orlando. In January 2001 Microsoft settled Sun's lawsuit, paying $20 million and agreeing to phase out its own Java. In December 2001 the standards body Ecma approved C# and the Common Language Infrastructure as ECMA-334 and ECMA-335. On 13 February 2002 version 1.0 shipped together with Visual Studio .NET. In June 2004 the free Mono project, led by Miguel de Icaza, reached version 1.0, so the same programs could run on Linux.`,facts:[`C# became an Ecma standard less than 18 months after its unveiling (2001).`,`Version 2.0 of the framework added generics in November 2005.`,`Microsoft made its .NET Core runtime open source in 2014.`]},"tech:webframework":{intro:`A web framework is a ready-made skeleton for a database-backed website. It has already decided where the code for each page goes, how web addresses map to that code, how records are read from and written to the database and how pages are built from templates, so a small team can have a working application in days.`,how:`Most frameworks split an application into models, which hold the data and its rules; views, which are the page templates; and controllers, which handle each request, the pattern called MVC. Rails follows 'convention over configuration': name a class Order and it reads the orders table without any setup file. Generators write starting code, migrations change the database step by step, and tests and protection against common attacks come built in.`,history:`In 2000 Apache Struts brought MVC to Java web applications, but it needed long XML configuration files. David Heinemeier Hansson extracted Ruby on Rails from Basecamp, a project-management site of the Chicago firm 37signals, and released it as open source in July 2004. In July 2005 Adrian Holovaty and Simon Willison released Django, built for the Lawrence Journal-World newspaper in Kansas and named after the guitarist Django Reinhardt. In 2005 Hansson's screencast of building a blog in 15 minutes drew crowds of programmers to Ruby, and in December 2005 Rails 1.0 was released. In 2006 Twitter and Shopify started on Rails, and in 2008 so did GitHub. In 2011 Laravel brought the same ideas to PHP.`,facts:[`Basecamp, the application Rails came from, launched in February 2004.`,`Rails 1.0 arrived in December 2005, 17 months after the first public release.`]},"tech:jslibs":{intro:`A JavaScript library is a small file of ready-made code that a web page loads to make scripting easier. It hides the differences between browsers behind one simple set of commands, such as find these elements, change them, animate them or fetch data from the server, so a few lines of script do what used to take dozens, and work everywhere.`,how:`jQuery is built around one function, written $, which takes a CSS selector such as '#menu li' and returns every matching element. Commands can then be chained: hide them, add a class, fade them back in. Inside, the library checks which browser it is running in and uses whatever method that browser supports for events, styles and background requests. Plug-ins written by others add sliders, date pickers and form checks on top.`,history:`In 2005 Sam Stephenson released Prototype, which shipped with Ruby on Rails, and Thomas Fuchs built the script.aculo.us effects library on top of it. In January 2006 John Resig announced jQuery at BarCamp in New York, and version 1.0 followed in August 2006. In February 2006 Yahoo published its own User Interface Library, YUI. In 2008 Google began hosting jQuery and other libraries on its servers for any site to use, and Microsoft and Nokia said they would ship jQuery with their developer tools. In 2009 Internet Explorer 8 added querySelectorAll, letting browsers themselves find elements by CSS selector and slowly reducing the need for such libraries.`,facts:[`jQuery was loaded by about 64% of all websites in October 2026.`,`Microsoft began shipping the open-source jQuery with Visual Studio in 2008.`]},"tech:restjson":{intro:`REST and JSON are a plain way for programs to exchange data over the web. Every piece of data, such as a user or an order, has its own web address, and a program reads or changes it with the web's ordinary commands. Answers come back as JSON: small, readable objects written in JavaScript's notation.`,how:`A REST interface treats data as resources with addresses like /users/42. A GET request reads one, POST creates one, PUT replaces it and DELETE removes it, and the web's standard status codes report success or failure. Each request carries everything the server needs, so the server keeps no memory of the conversation and answers can be cached. JSON writes data as nested lists and name-value pairs, which any language can read and JavaScript can use directly.`,history:`In 2000 Roy Fielding, a co-author of the web's HTTP standard, described REST, Representational State Transfer, in his PhD dissertation at the University of California, Irvine. In April 2001 Douglas Crockford and Chip Morningstar sent the first JSON message at their start-up, State Software, and in 2002 Crockford put the format's grammar on json.org. In 2004 Flickr, and in 2006 Twitter, opened simple web APIs on which outside developers built whole products. In July 2006 JSON was written up for the internet's engineers as RFC 4627. In April 2010 Facebook's Graph API gave every person, page and photo an address that returned JSON.`,facts:[`Tim O'Reilly reported that about 85% of Amazon web-service use was REST-style, not SOAP (2003).`,`By May 2011, 55% of new web APIs offered JSON (ProgrammableWeb).`]},"tech:scipy":{intro:`Scientific Python joins an easy, readable programming language to fast mathematics libraries written in C and Fortran. Scientists write short scripts in Python, while the heavy number crunching on large grids of numbers runs at the speed of compiled code, giving them the convenience of MATLAB without its licence fees.`,how:`NumPy's core is the array: a block of numbers of one type laid side by side in memory, with any number of dimensions. One statement such as a * b + c works on every element at once, running in a tight loop in C instead of Python, and broadcasting stretches smaller arrays to fit larger ones. On top sit SciPy for statistics, signal processing and optimisation, and Matplotlib for charts. Linear algebra calls the tuned BLAS and LAPACK libraries that supercomputer users rely on.`,history:`In 1995 Jim Hugunin wrote Numeric, the first array package for Python. In 2001 Travis Oliphant, Eric Jones and Pearu Peterson released the SciPy library, and Fernando Pérez started IPython, an interactive shell for exploring data. In 2003 John Hunter released Matplotlib, so Python could draw charts fit for scientific papers. Rival array packages, Numeric and the newer Numarray from the Space Telescope Science Institute, split the community until Oliphant merged them into NumPy in 2005, with NumPy 1.0 following in 2006. In 2008 Wes McKinney began pandas for tables of financial data, and in 2010 scikit-learn put machine learning a few lines away. In October 2021 Python topped the TIOBE index of language popularity for the first time.`,facts:[`Python ranked 8th on the TIOBE index in 2006 and 1st in 2026.`,`NumPy helped produce the first image of a black hole (2019).`]},"tech:html5":{intro:`HTML5 is a new edition of the web's page language, written for applications as much as for documents. The browser itself learns to play video and sound, draw graphics and games on a canvas, store data on the user's computer and keep working offline, so the open web can do what used to need plug-ins.`,how:`New tags do the work directly: video and audio elements play media with built-in controls, and the canvas element gives scripts a surface to paint pixels on, fast enough for games. Local storage keeps data in the browser, an offline cache lets a web app start with no connection, and new form fields check dates and email addresses by themselves. Unlike earlier specifications, HTML5 also spells out exactly how browsers must handle badly written pages, so they all behave alike.`,history:`In 2004 Apple invented the canvas element for the Dashboard widgets in its Safari browser. In June 2004, after the W3C chose to concentrate on the stricter XHTML, Apple, Mozilla and Opera formed the WHATWG to extend HTML instead, with Ian Hickson as editor. On 22 January 2008 the W3C published the first public draft of HTML5. In January 2010 YouTube began testing an HTML5 video player, and in April 2010 Steve Jobs's 'Thoughts on Flash' argued that HTML5 made plug-ins unnecessary. In 2010 browser makers fought over video formats, as Google backed its free WebM against the patented H.264 that Apple and Microsoft favoured. On 28 October 2014 HTML5 became a W3C recommendation.`,facts:[`The iPad launched without Flash, relying on HTML5 for web video (April 2010).`,`YouTube made HTML5 video its default in January 2015.`]},"tech:responsive":{intro:`Responsive web design builds one website that rearranges itself to fit whatever screen shows it. On a wide monitor the page spreads into several columns; on a phone the same page stacks into a single column with bigger buttons, and pictures shrink to fit, with no separate mobile site to keep up to date.`,how:`It rests on three techniques. A fluid grid sizes columns in percentages instead of fixed pixels, so the layout stretches. Flexible images are told never to grow wider than their box. CSS media queries apply different rules depending on the screen's width, so at a chosen breakpoint a three-column layout becomes one. A viewport tag tells phone browsers to use the real screen width instead of pretending to be a desktop.`,history:`In 2007 the iPhone's Safari browser showed full desktop pages shrunk to fit, and Apple introduced the viewport tag so sites could ask for phone-sized layouts. On 25 May 2010 Ethan Marcotte named and described responsive web design in the online magazine A List Apart. In 2011 Marcotte expanded the idea into a short book, and Twitter's Mark Otto and Jacob Thornton released the Bootstrap toolkit. In January 2012 Bootstrap 2 made responsive grids standard for thousands of sites. In June 2012 media queries became a W3C recommendation. In October 2016 phones and tablets overtook desktop computers in worldwide web use, by StatCounter's count.`,facts:[`Marcotte's article appeared on 25 May 2010.`,`Google began ranking mobile-friendly pages higher on phones on 21 April 2015, nicknamed Mobilegeddon.`]},"tech:spa":{intro:`A single-page application is a website that loads once and then behaves like a desktop program. Instead of fetching a fresh page from the server for every click, the browser keeps one page open and a JavaScript framework redraws parts of it, asking the server only for data.`,how:`The framework splits the screen into views tied to models of the data. When the user clicks, a router changes the address in the browser bar and swaps the view without a reload, so the back button still works. Data arrives as JSON from the server. AngularJS adds two-way binding, so editing a form field updates the model and the model updates every place that shows it. The costs are a heavy first download and pages that search engines struggle to read.`,history:`In 2004 Gmail showed that a whole email program could live inside one web page. In 2008 Apple built its MobileMe web apps with the SproutCore framework. In July 2010 Steve Sanderson released Knockout, and on 13 October 2010 Jeremy Ashkenas released Backbone.js. In October 2010 Google released AngularJS, begun by Miško Hevery as a side project, and in June 2012 it reached version 1.0. In 2011 Ember.js grew out of SproutCore under Yehuda Katz. In September 2016 Google released Angular 2, a complete rewrite that could not run the original's code.`,facts:[`Backbone.js was released on 13 October 2010.`,`Google ended support for the original AngularJS on 31 December 2021.`]},"tech:declui":{intro:`Declarative component UI means describing what the screen should look like for the data you have, and letting a library work out what to change. The page is built from components, small reusable pieces such as a comment box or a button, each a function that turns data into a description of the screen.`,how:`When data changes, a React component simply renders again, producing a new virtual tree of elements in memory. React compares it with the previous tree, a step called diffing, and touches the real page only where they differ, which keeps updates fast. Data flows one way, from parent components down to children, so it is easy to tell why the screen looks as it does. JSX lets programmers write markup inside JavaScript, a mix many find shocking.`,history:`In 2011 Facebook engineer Jordan Walke built an early prototype called FaxJS, and React began running Facebook's News Feed that year. In 2012 Instagram's website was built with it after Facebook bought the company. On 29 May 2013 Facebook released React as open source at JSConf US, where many developers disliked mixing markup with code. In February 2014 Evan You released Vue, and in 2015 Dan Abramov's Redux offered a popular way to manage an application's data. In September 2017 Facebook moved React to the MIT licence after companies objected to a patent clause in the old one. In February 2019 hooks let components keep state as plain functions.`,facts:[`React Native carried the same model to phone apps in 2015.`,`Svelte, which compiles components away instead of diffing, appeared on 29 November 2016.`]},"tech:webdesktop":{intro:`Desktop apps in web technology carry a whole browser engine inside a program, so code written in HTML, CSS and JavaScript runs as a normal application with its own window, menus and icon. A team that has built a web product can turn it into Windows, Mac and Linux programs from the same code.`,how:`The app bundles Chromium, the open-source core of Google's Chrome, to draw its windows, and Node.js, which lets JavaScript read files, open network connections and use other system features a web page cannot. A main process creates the windows, and each window runs a page that talks to it by messages. The cost is size: every app carries its own browser, so even a simple tool needs a large download and plenty of memory.`,history:`In 1999 Microsoft's Internet Explorer 5 could already run HTML Applications, web pages with the rights of a desktop program. In 2008 Adobe AIR let web and Flash developers build desktop apps. In 2011 Roger Wang at Intel's open-source centre in China started node-webkit, joining Node.js to a browser engine. In 2013 Cheng Zhao at GitHub built Atom Shell to run GitHub's new Atom code editor, which went public in February 2014. In April 2015 Atom Shell was renamed Electron, and that month Microsoft previewed Visual Studio Code, built on it. By 2016 the desktop apps of Slack and Discord used it too.`,facts:[`Electron 1.0 was released in May 2016.`,`GitHub retired Atom, the editor Electron was built for, in December 2022.`]},"tech:microservices":{intro:`Microservices is a way of building a large system as many small programs instead of one giant one. Each service does one business job, such as payments, search or recommendations, keeps its own data and talks to the others over the network. Each is owned by one small team, which can change and release it without waiting for anyone else.`,how:`Services call each other through APIs, usually REST over HTTP or messages on a queue, and each runs in its own containers so it can be scaled on its own. Because no service reaches into another's database, teams can choose their own languages and release many times a day. The price is complexity: a single click may cross dozens of services, failures can cascade, and data that once sat in one database must be kept consistent across many.`,history:`In 1968 Melvin Conway observed that a system's design copies the structure of the organisation that builds it, the rule known as Conway's law. Around 2002 Jeff Bezos ordered every Amazon team to expose its data and functions only through service interfaces. In August 2008 a corrupted database stopped Netflix's DVD shipments for three days, and the company began rebuilding itself as many small services on Amazon's cloud. In May 2011 a workshop of software architects near Venice discussed the style, and in 2012 they settled on the name microservices. On 25 March 2014 James Lewis and Martin Fowler published an article defining it, which spread through the industry. In 2023 an Amazon Prime Video team cut one monitoring system's costs by over 90% by merging its services back into a single program.`,facts:[`Netflix finished moving to cloud microservices in January 2016, more than seven years after starting.`,`Lewis and Fowler's article listed nine common characteristics of microservices (2014).`]},"tech:modernapplang":{intro:`Modern app languages are new programming languages for phone apps, designed so that the compiler refuses many of the mistakes that used to crash programs. They are shorter and easier to read than the older languages they replace, yet work alongside them, so a team can move one file at a time.`,how:`The biggest change is null safety. In the older languages any object reference may secretly be empty, and using an empty one crashes the app. Here an ordinary type can never be empty; a value that might be missing gets a question mark, as in String?, and the compiler insists that the code checks it first. Type inference, value types and short closures cut boilerplate, and Kotlin compiles to the same bytecode as Java, so the two mix freely.`,history:`In 1965 Tony Hoare added the null reference to the ALGOL W language, simply because it was easy to implement. In July 2011 JetBrains, the tools company from St Petersburg, unveiled Kotlin, named after an island near the city. On 2 June 2014 Apple surprised its developers by announcing Swift, created by Chris Lattner, as a successor to Objective-C. In December 2015 Apple made Swift open source, and in February 2016 Kotlin reached version 1.0. In May 2017 Google announced official support for Kotlin on Android, and in May 2019 it declared Kotlin its preferred language.`,facts:[`Hoare called the null reference his 'billion-dollar mistake' (2009).`,`Swift became open source on 3 December 2015.`,`Kotlin 1.0 came nearly five years after its unveiling (2016).`]},"tech:lowcode":{intro:`Low-code platforms let people build business applications mostly by drawing them: lay out the screens, sketch the workflow as boxes and arrows, connect a data source and press publish. A little code fills the gaps, so staff outside the programming department can build the forms, approvals and dashboards they need.`,how:`The platform runs in the cloud and supplies the parts every business app needs: a database, user logins, forms, a workflow engine and connectors to common systems such as Salesforce or SAP. A visual model describes the app, and the platform generates or interprets it and hosts the result on the web and on phones. The catch, as with earlier tools, is that unusual requirements are hard to express, and the apps run only on that vendor's platform.`,history:`In 1987 HyperCard on the Macintosh, and in 1992 Microsoft Access, let non-programmers build small applications of their own. In 2001 OutSystems was founded in Lisbon, and in 2005 Mendix in Rotterdam, to build applications from visual models. In 2008 Salesforce launched Force.com so that customers could build their own apps on its cloud. On 9 June 2014 the research firm Forrester gave the field its name, low-code. In 2016 Microsoft launched PowerApps, and in 2018 Siemens bought Mendix for about $730 million.`,facts:[`OutSystems was valued at $9.5 billion (2021).`,`Gartner put spending on low-code tools at $26.9 billion, up 19.6% (2023).`]},"tech:crossmobile":{intro:`Cross-platform mobile UI frameworks let a team write one app and run it on both iPhone and Android. Instead of two separate apps in two languages, one code base describes the screens, and the framework turns them into fast, native-feeling interfaces on each kind of phone.`,how:`React Native runs the app's JavaScript in a separate engine and sends instructions across a bridge to create real native buttons, lists and text views, so the app uses each platform's own controls. Flutter goes the other way: apps are written in Dart, and Flutter draws every pixel itself with its own graphics engine, like a game, so screens look identical everywhere. Both can reload a changed screen on a running phone within seconds, without rebuilding the app.`,history:`In 2008 PhoneGap began wrapping web pages inside native app shells, and in 2011 Xamarin let C# programmers write apps for both platforms. In January 2015 Facebook announced React Native, and released it as open source for iPhone in March 2015 and for Android that September. In April 2015 Google showed an experiment called Sky, which became Flutter, at its Dart developer summit. In 2016 Microsoft bought Xamarin and made its tools free. In June 2018 Airbnb announced it was moving away from React Native, and on 4 December 2018 Google released Flutter 1.0.`,facts:[`Flutter 1.0 launched at an event in London on 4 December 2018.`,`Airbnb explained its retreat from React Native in a five-part blog series (2018).`]},"tech:clouddesign":{intro:`Multiplayer design tools put a professional design program in a browser tab. Designers draw app and website screens there, and everyone on the team can open the same file at once, seeing each other's cursors and edits appear live, as in a shared document. Engineers read sizes and colours straight from the file.`,how:`Figma's drawing engine is written in C++ and compiled to run inside the browser, drawing through the graphics card with WebGL, so it can be as fast as a desktop program. The file lives on Figma's servers, which merge everyone's changes as they happen, so there is no 'final_v7' file to email around. Comments, version history and a link anyone can open replace exported pictures, and shared components keep buttons and colours consistent across hundreds of screens.`,history:`In 2010 Bohemian Coding's Sketch, a Mac-only program, began replacing Photoshop for designing app screens. In 2012 Dylan Field and Evan Wallace, friends from Brown University, founded Figma in San Francisco to build design software for the browser. In December 2015 Figma opened a closed preview, and on 27 September 2016 it launched to everyone, free for individual designers. In September 2022 Adobe agreed to buy Figma for about $20 billion. In December 2023 Adobe abandoned the deal under pressure from European and British regulators and paid Figma a $1 billion break fee. In July 2025 Figma listed its shares on the New York Stock Exchange.`,facts:[`Figma's revenue grew from $505 million (2023) to $749 million (2024).`,`Switching its engine to WebAssembly cut Figma's load time by three times (2017).`]},"tech:wasm":{intro:`WebAssembly is a compact machine code for the web. Programs written in C, C++ or Rust can be compiled into it and run inside any browser at close to their normal speed, in the same safe sandbox as JavaScript, so heavy software such as games, design tools and video editors can live in a browser tab.`,how:`A WebAssembly module is a binary file of instructions for an imaginary stack machine, designed to be decoded and checked quickly. The browser validates it, then compiles it to the real processor's code. The module gets one block of linear memory and can touch nothing else; it reaches the page, the network and files only through functions that JavaScript hands it. Because it skips JavaScript's guesswork about types, its speed is steady and predictable.`,history:`In 2010 Alon Zakai began Emscripten, a compiler that turned C and C++ programs into JavaScript. In 2011 Google shipped Native Client in Chrome, a way to run sandboxed native code that other browsers refused to adopt. In 2013 Mozilla defined asm.js, a strict subset of JavaScript its engine could compile ahead of time, and showed the Unreal game engine running in Firefox. On 17 June 2015 Mozilla, Google, Microsoft and Apple announced WebAssembly together. In March 2017 Firefox 52 and Chrome 57 shipped the first version, and Safari and Edge followed later in 2017. On 5 December 2019 the W3C made WebAssembly an official web standard.`,facts:[`All four major browser makers backed WebAssembly from its announcement (2015).`,`The W3C called it the fourth language of the web, after HTML, CSS and JavaScript (2019).`]},"tech:nativedecl":{intro:`Declarative native UI is the phone makers' own version of React's idea. Instead of creating buttons and labels and changing them by hand whenever the data changes, the programmer writes a function that describes the screen for the current data, and the system works out what to redraw. Apple and Google build it into their official toolkits.`,how:`In SwiftUI a view is a small structure whose body lists its contents, such as a line of text above a button, and properties marked @State tell the system which values the view depends on. When one changes, the body runs again and the framework updates only what differs. Jetpack Compose does the same with Kotlin functions marked @Composable. Live previews show each screen as it is written, and the new toolkits can sit inside older apps.`,history:`In 2008 Apple's iPhone SDK and Google's Android both built screens from objects that programmers changed step by step, with layouts drawn in Interface Builder or written in XML. In 2013 React showed web developers the declarative style, and in December 2018 Google's Flutter brought it to phones. In May 2019 Google previewed Jetpack Compose at its I/O conference. On 3 June 2019 Apple announced SwiftUI at its WWDC conference, and it shipped with iOS 13 that September. In July 2021 Compose reached version 1.0.`,facts:[`SwiftUI apps needed iOS 13 or later (2019).`,`Compose 1.0 came about two years after its first preview (2021).`]},"tech:debugger":{intro:`An interactive debugger lets a programmer stop a running program at any point, look inside the computer's memory, change it and carry on, all from the console. Instead of studying a printout of the whole memory after a crash, the programmer can watch the program work step by step and fix it while it waits.`,how:`The programmer sets a breakpoint by naming an address, and the debugger quietly replaces the instruction there with a jump into itself. When the program reaches it, control returns to the programmer, who can examine any memory word, shown as a number or as an instruction with its symbolic name, deposit a new value, patch an instruction, step one instruction at a time, or let the program run on. The debugger lives in memory beside the program, ready at any moment.`,history:`On 9 September 1947 operators of the Harvard Mark II taped a moth found in a relay into their logbook as the 'first actual case of bug being found'. Around 1959 Jack Dennis and Thomas Stockham wrote FLIT for MIT's TX-0, letting users examine and change memory from a typewriter. In 1961 MIT hackers including Alan Kotok wrote DDT for the new PDP-1, its name a joke on the insect spray. In 1967 DDT became the command shell of MIT's Incompatible Timesharing System, so users ran every program from inside the debugger. In 1974 Gary Kildall's CP/M for microcomputers came with its own DDT. In 1986 Richard Stallman released GDB, which showed the program's own source lines and variable names.`,facts:[`The Harvard moth was logged at 15:45 on 9 September 1947.`,`A PDP-1, the machine DDT was written for, cost about $120,000 (1960).`]},"tech:modules":{intro:`Information hiding is a rule for cutting a large program into modules. Instead of splitting it by the steps it performs, the designer lists the decisions most likely to change, such as how data is stored or which device is used, and hides each one inside its own module, behind a small interface that the rest of the program uses.`,how:`Each module offers a few operations, such as 'add a word' or 'fetch line n', and keeps its data structures and methods secret. Other modules may call only those operations, so when the hidden decision changes, only one module needs rewriting. A specification describes what each operation does without saying how. Teams can work in parallel, modules can be tested alone, and a change no longer ripples through the whole system.`,history:`In May 1972 David Parnas, then at Carnegie Mellon University, published a method for specifying modules by their outside behaviour alone. In December 1972 his paper 'On the Criteria To Be Used in Decomposing Systems into Modules' compared two designs of the same small indexing program and showed that hiding decisions made the second far easier to change. In 1974 Barbara Liskov's CLU language built abstract data types into the language itself. In 1978 Niklaus Wirth's Modula-2 gave each module a separate definition part listing what it exported. In 1983 Ada, designed for the US Department of Defense, made packages with public and private parts a standard feature. In 2008 Liskov received the Turing Award, partly for this work on data abstraction.`,facts:[`Parnas's landmark paper ran to just six pages (1972).`,`Ada became an American standard in 1983 and an ISO standard in 1987.`]},"tech:funcprog":{intro:`Functional programming builds programs from functions that take values and return new values without changing anything along the way. Since no variable can be altered behind a programmer's back, a function always gives the same answer for the same input, which makes programs easier to reason about, to test and even to prove correct.`,how:`Instead of loops that update counters, functional programs use recursion and functions that apply other functions to whole lists, such as map, which transforms every element. Data is immutable: changing a list really makes a new one that shares most of the old. ML adds a type system that works out the type of every expression by itself, catching mistakes without the programmer writing types down, and pattern matching takes data apart case by case.`,history:`In 1936 Alonzo Church used the lambda calculus, a mathematics built entirely from functions, to explore what can be computed. In 1958 John McCarthy's LISP brought functions as values into a working programming language. In 1973 Robin Milner's team at the University of Edinburgh created ML as the language for steering the LCF theorem prover. In 1977 John Backus, the creator of FORTRAN, used his Turing Award lecture to attack the step-by-step 'von Neumann style' and argue for functional programming. In 1986 Joe Armstrong, Robert Virding and Mike Williams at Ericsson began Erlang, for telephone switches that must never stop. On 1 April 1990 a committee of researchers released Haskell 1.0, a lazy, purely functional language.`,facts:[`Milner received the Turing Award in 1991, partly for ML.`,`WhatsApp handled 2 million connections on a single Erlang server (2012).`]},"tech:hwdebug":{intro:`Logic analyzers and in-circuit emulators are the tools for bringing a new microprocessor board to life. A logic analyzer records dozens of digital signals at once and shows what happened on every wire; an in-circuit emulator takes the place of the board's processor, so the engineer can stop it, look inside and change its program.`,how:`An oscilloscope shows two or four signals as wavy lines; a logic analyzer clips onto 16, 32 or more wires, records each as a 1 or a 0 on every clock tick, and waits for a trigger, such as one address on the bus, before filling its memory. It shows the capture as a timing chart or a list of bus transfers. An emulator's cable plugs into the empty processor socket, and a processor inside the emulator runs the program, with breakpoints and a trace of recent instructions.`,history:`In 1973 Hewlett-Packard's 5000A and Biomation's timing analyzers created a new kind of instrument for digital circuits. In 1975 Intel sold the ICE-80 in-circuit emulator for its 8080 chip as part of the Intellec MDS-800 development system. By the early 1980s independent firms sold emulators for almost every popular microprocessor and microcontroller. By the late 1980s processors ran too fast for a long cable to the socket, and chips were packed so densely that probes were hard to attach. In 1990 the JTAG standard began building test and debug access into the chips themselves.`,facts:[`Intel's ICE-80 plugged into the 8080's 40-pin socket (1975).`,`An MDS-800 with emulator boards cost about $20,000 (1975).`]},"tech:inspections":{intro:`A code inspection is a formal meeting in which a few programmers read a design or a piece of code line by line, looking for mistakes before anything is run. Each defect found by reading is fixed for the price of an edit, instead of being hunted down later in testing or by customers.`,how:`Each inspector has a role. The moderator runs the meeting, the reader paraphrases the code aloud, the author answers questions and a tester thinks about how it could fail. Everyone studies the material in advance with a checklist of common errors. The meeting only finds and logs defects, never debates fixes, and is kept short. Afterwards the author reworks the code, the moderator checks every fix, and the defect counts show where the process itself needs improving.`,history:`In 1971 Gerald Weinberg's book 'The Psychology of Computer Programming' urged 'egoless programming', in which programmers invite others to read their code. In 1972 Michael Fagan began trying out structured inspections at IBM's laboratory in Kingston, New York. In 1976 he published the method in the IBM Systems Journal, with figures showing that inspections found most defects before the first test. In 1977 Edward Yourdon's book on structured walkthroughs spread a lighter version of the idea. In 1986 Fagan published an update reporting results from many companies. In 2008 GitHub's pull requests and the Gerrit review tool moved code review onto the web, attached to every change.`,facts:[`Studies of the 1976 method credited inspections with finding 60 to 90% of defects before testing.`,`A study at Cisco found reviewers worked best on 200 to 400 lines at a time (2006).`]},"tech:staticanalysis":{intro:`Static analysis tools read a program's source code without running it and point at lines that are probably wrong: a variable used before it is given a value, a function called with the wrong arguments, a file opened and never closed. They catch mistakes the compiler lets through, before the program reaches testing.`,how:`A checker parses the code as a compiler would, then applies rules. Simple rules look at one line or one function. Stronger tools follow every path through the program, tracking what each variable might hold, to find a pointer that can be empty on one rare branch or a lock that is never released. The art is precision: a tool that cries wolf with too many false alarms is soon ignored.`,history:`In 1977 Patrick and Radhia Cousot in France published abstract interpretation, a mathematical theory for working out what a program can do without running it. On 26 July 1978 Stephen Johnson of Bell Labs described lint, named after the fluff picked off clothes, which checked C programs for errors the compiler ignored. In 1979 lint shipped with Version 7 Unix. In 2002 Dawson Engler's group at Stanford, whose checkers had found hundreds of bugs in Linux, founded Coverity to sell them. In 2003 the Astrée analyser proved that the flight-control software of the Airbus A340 could never hit a run-time error. In 2014 Synopsys bought Coverity, and in 2015 Facebook released its own checker, Infer, as open source.`,facts:[`Astrée checked 132,000 lines of Airbus flight-control C with no false alarms (2003).`,`Synopsys paid about $375 million for Coverity (2014).`]},"tech:tui":{intro:`Full-screen text interfaces turn an ordinary character terminal into a canvas. Instead of printing line after line, a program draws boxes, menus, forms and highlighted bars anywhere on the screen using letters and line-drawing characters, and the user moves around with the arrow keys, so it feels like operating a machine rather than typing commands.`,how:`Every terminal model has its own escape codes for moving the cursor and clearing the screen, so the curses library reads a database describing each one. The program draws into an image of the screen kept in memory; when it calls refresh, curses works out the fewest characters needed to make the real screen match, which matters on slow serial lines. On PCs, programs skip the terminal entirely and write characters and colours straight into video memory.`,history:`In 1978 Bill Joy at Berkeley built termcap, a database of terminal capabilities, for his vi editor. In 1978 DEC's VT100 terminal popularised a standard set of ANSI cursor codes. Around 1980 Ken Arnold wrote the curses library at Berkeley, and Michael Toy and Glenn Wichman used it for Rogue, a dungeon game drawn in letters. In January 1983 Lotus 1-2-3 for the IBM PC showed how fast a text screen could be when written directly to video memory. In 1990 Borland's Turbo Vision, with Turbo Pascal 6.0, offered windows, menus and dialog boxes in text mode, complete with mouse support. In 1993 ncurses, a free version of curses, appeared for Linux and other free systems.`,facts:[`The IBM PC's text screen held 80 columns by 25 rows (1981).`,`The PC's character set included 40 box-drawing characters (1981).`]},"tech:profiler":{intro:`A profiler measures where a program spends its time. It counts how often each function is called and how long it takes, so programmers can speed up the parts that actually matter. Nearly always the slow part turns out not to be the one they guessed.`,how:`Two techniques combine. Sampling interrupts the program many times a second and notes which instruction is running; over a long run the samples show which functions take the most time. Instrumentation adds a little code to every function to count calls and record who called whom. gprof joins the two into a call graph, so a function that is cheap by itself but calls expensive ones gets the blame it deserves.`,history:`In 1971 Donald Knuth studied real FORTRAN programs and found that a tiny fraction of the code accounted for most of the running time. In 1974 he warned that 'premature optimization is the root of all evil'. In June 1982 Susan Graham, Peter Kessler and Marshall McKusick of Berkeley presented gprof, which charted time across the whole call graph. In 1983 gprof shipped with 4.2BSD Unix. In 1997 Intel's VTune read counters built into the Pentium processor to show cache misses and stalls, not only time. In 2011 Brendan Gregg invented flame graphs, which draw millions of samples as one picture of where the time goes.`,facts:[`Knuth found that under 4% of a program usually takes over half its run time (1971).`,`Linux's own profiler, perf, arrived in 2009.`]},"tech:jtag":{intro:`JTAG adds a small test and debug port to every chip on a circuit board. Through four or five extra pins, a tester can check every connection on the board without touching it with probes, and an engineer can stop a running processor, inspect its registers and load new software into its memory.`,how:`Each pin of a JTAG chip has a boundary-scan cell, and the cells are linked into one long shift register. A test controller clocks a pattern into the chain, sets every output pin of one chip, then reads what arrives at the neighbouring chips' inputs, proving the solder joints between them. The same port carries debug commands into processor cores, and many chips on one board can share one chain.`,history:`In 1985 European electronics makers including Philips formed the Joint European Test Action Group, which became JTAG when North American companies joined. By the late 1980s surface-mount chips with closely spaced pins were making bed-of-nails testers, which pressed probes onto every point of a board, impractical. In 1989 Intel's 80486 processor already included a JTAG test port. In 1990 the IEEE approved the work as standard 1149.1, the Test Access Port and Boundary-Scan Architecture. In 1994 ARM's EmbeddedICE used the port to give processor cores breakpoints without an external emulator. In 2004 ARM's CoreSight let debuggers trace a running chip's instructions through the same pins.`,facts:[`The basic JTAG port needs four signals: data in, data out, clock and mode select (1990).`,`IEEE 1149.1 was revised in 2001 and again in 2013.`]},"tech:memcheck":{intro:`Memory checkers watch a program as it runs and catch the memory mistakes that languages like C and C++ allow: reading past the end of an array, using memory after freeing it, reading memory never set and forgetting to free blocks at all. They turn silent corruption, which may crash the program much later, into a clear report at the moment it happens.`,how:`The checker keeps a shadow record of every byte of memory: whether it is allocated, freed or written yet. Purify inserts checking code around every load and store in the compiled program. Small red zones of forbidden bytes are placed around each allocated block, and freed memory is held back for a while, so overruns and late use land on marked bytes and are reported with the exact line of code. At exit, blocks nobody points to any more are listed as leaks.`,history:`In October 1991 Reed Hastings founded Pure Software in California, and in 1992 he and Bob Joyce described Purify, which checked every memory access of a compiled program. In 1996 Pure merged with Atria, and in 1997 Rational Software bought the combined company, the year Hastings co-founded Netflix. In 2002 Julian Seward released Valgrind, a free checker for Linux that ran programs on a simulated processor. In 2012 Google's AddressSanitizer, built into the compiler, made checking fast enough to run on every test, slowing programs by about 73%. In 2019 Microsoft engineers reported that about 70% of the security flaws it fixed each year were memory-safety bugs.`,facts:[`AddressSanitizer found over 300 new bugs in the Chromium browser (2012).`,`Valgrind's Memcheck typically ran programs 10 to 50 times slower (2002).`]},"tech:patterns":{intro:`A design pattern is a named, proven solution to a problem that keeps coming up in object-oriented design, such as how to let many objects react when one changes, or how to create objects without fixing their exact class. Naming the solutions gives a team a shared vocabulary: 'make it an Observer' says in four words what used to take a whiteboard.`,how:`Each pattern is written up in the same form: its name, the problem and when it applies, the structure of classes and objects that solves it, the consequences and trade-offs, and sample code. The classic catalogue sorts its patterns into three families: creational ones such as Factory Method and Singleton, structural ones such as Adapter and Composite, and behavioural ones such as Observer, Strategy and Iterator. Patterns are not libraries to download but ideas that each program builds in its own way.`,history:`In 1977 the architect Christopher Alexander published 'A Pattern Language', a catalogue of proven solutions for towns and buildings. In 1987 Kent Beck and Ward Cunningham presented pattern languages for programs at the OOPSLA conference. In October 1994 Erich Gamma, Richard Helm, Ralph Johnson and John Vlissides, soon nicknamed the Gang of Four, published 'Design Patterns' at OOPSLA in Portland. In March 1995 Cunningham built the first wiki, the Portland Pattern Repository, so that programmers could write up patterns together. In 1996 Peter Norvig showed that 16 of the 23 patterns became simpler or vanished in dynamic languages such as Lisp. In 2004 'Head First Design Patterns' taught the catalogue to a new generation of Java programmers.`,facts:[`The book's 23 patterns split into 5 creational, 7 structural and 11 behavioural (1994).`,`Alexander's 253 patterns ran from the shape of regions down to window seats (1977).`]},"tech:drivers":{intro:`A driver model is an operating system's standard way of letting hardware makers supply the small programs, called drivers, that control their devices. With Plug and Play, the system notices a new card or device by itself, works out what it is, gives it the resources it needs and loads the right driver, with no switches to set on the card.`,how:`Every Plug and Play device reports an identity, such as a vendor and a device number. At start-up the system walks each bus, collects these identities and hands out interrupt lines, memory and input-output addresses so that no two devices clash. It then matches each identity against installation files supplied by the makers and loads the driver, which talks to the system through a fixed set of calls. Drivers run inside the kernel with full rights, so one buggy driver can crash the whole machine.`,history:`In 1992 Intel's PCI bus let expansion cards describe themselves to the computer, ending much of the fiddling with jumpers. In 1993 Microsoft and Intel published a Plug and Play specification for the older ISA cards. On 24 August 1995 Windows 95 brought Plug and Play to millions of PCs, though users joked about 'plug and pray'. In 1995 Linux gained loadable kernel modules, so drivers could be added without rebuilding the kernel. In 1996 USB arrived, letting devices be plugged in and removed while the computer runs. In April 1998 a public demonstration of Windows 98 crashed on stage when a scanner was plugged in, with Bill Gates beside it.`,facts:[`Drivers caused 85% of recently reported Windows XP failures, a study found (2003).`,`Windows 98 and Windows 2000 shared one Windows Driver Model, so a driver could serve both (1998).`]},"tech:refactoring":{intro:`Refactoring is improving the inside of a program without changing what it does. A programmer renames a confusing variable, pulls a repeated block into its own function or splits a class that does too much, in tiny steps, running the tests after each one, so the program never stops working while its design gets better.`,how:`Each refactoring is a small, named transformation with a recipe: Extract Method, Rename Field, Move Method, Replace Conditional with Polymorphism. Because every step is tiny and the tests run between steps, any mistake shows at once and is easy to undo. Programmers look for code smells, such as long methods or duplicated code, as signs that a refactoring is due. Tools that understand the code can perform the common ones automatically and safely.`,history:`In 1990 William Opdyke and Ralph Johnson at the University of Illinois used the word refactoring in a paper on restructuring object-oriented programs. In 1992 Opdyke's PhD thesis showed how each refactoring could be checked to preserve a program's behaviour. Around 1997 John Brant and Don Roberts at Illinois built the Refactoring Browser for Smalltalk, a tool that performed refactorings automatically. In 1999 Martin Fowler's book 'Refactoring: Improving the Design of Existing Code' catalogued the moves, with a chapter on code smells written with Kent Beck. In 2001 JetBrains' IntelliJ IDEA and IBM's Eclipse brought automated refactoring to everyday Java programming. In 2018 Fowler published a second edition with its examples in JavaScript.`,facts:[`Fowler's catalogue named more than 70 refactorings (1999).`,`IntelliJ IDEA 1.0 shipped in January 2001.`]},"tech:crashreport":{intro:`Crash reporting from the field means that when a program crashes on a customer's computer, it offers to send a short report home to its maker. Gathered by the million and sorted by cause, the reports show which few bugs hurt the most people, so those can be fixed first.`,how:`When a program fails, a small handler captures a minidump: which code was running, the call stack and a little memory, a few hundred kilobytes instead of the whole program. With the user's permission it is sent to the maker's servers, which group reports into buckets by program, version and exact place of the crash. Engineers work down the list from the biggest bucket, and once a fix exists the server can point the user to it.`,history:`In 1992 Dr Watson in Windows 3.1 recorded details of crashes in a log file that users could send to support staff. In October 2001 Windows XP introduced Windows Error Reporting, with its 'Send Error Report' button. In October 2002 Steve Ballmer told customers that the reports had shown how few bugs caused most crashes. In 2007 Google released its Breakpad crash reporter as open source, and in 2008 Firefox 3 adopted it. In 2009 Microsoft researchers published ten years of lessons from their error reporting system. In January 2013 Twitter bought Crashlytics, a crash reporter for phone apps.`,facts:[`Fixing 20% of reported bugs removed 80% of errors; 1% of bugs caused half (Microsoft, 2002).`,`Windows Error Reporting ran on about 1 billion computers (2009).`]},"tech:tdd":{intro:`Test-driven development is a way of writing code in which the test comes first. Before writing a piece of code, the programmer writes a small automated test that fails because the code does not exist yet, then writes just enough to make it pass, then tidies up. The program grows in tiny, always-tested steps.`,how:`The cycle is often called red, green, refactor. Red: write a test for the next small behaviour and watch it fail, which proves the test works. Green: write the simplest code that passes. Refactor: clean up the code and the test while everything stays green. A cycle takes minutes. The result is a large suite of fast tests that acts as a safety net for later changes and as a working description of what the code should do.`,history:`In 1994 Kent Beck wrote SUnit, a small testing framework for Smalltalk. In 1997 Beck and Erich Gamma wrote JUnit for Java, by their account on a flight to the OOPSLA conference. In 1999 Beck's 'Extreme Programming Explained' made writing tests first one of its core practices. In November 2002 his book 'Test-Driven Development: By Example' gave the method its name and its rules. In 2008 a study by Nachiappan Nagappan and colleagues found that teams at Microsoft and IBM using it shipped far fewer defects but took longer. In April 2014 David Heinemeier Hansson, creator of Rails, declared 'TDD is dead', starting a public debate with Beck and Martin Fowler.`,facts:[`Teams using TDD had 40 to 90% fewer pre-release defects, for 15 to 35% more time (2008).`,`By 2000 JUnit had inspired copies for many languages, known together as xUnit.`]},"tech:ddd":{intro:`Domain-driven design is a way of building business software around a model of the business itself. Programmers and business experts agree on one shared language, so that an order, a shipment or a refund means the same thing in meetings and in the code, and the code uses exactly those words.`,how:`The team builds a model of the domain in a ubiquitous language and keeps the code faithful to it. A large system is split into bounded contexts, each with its own model; a customer in billing need not match a customer in shipping, and translators sit at the borders. Inside a context, entities have an identity, value objects such as an amount of money do not, and aggregates group objects that must change together. Repositories store and fetch aggregates, keeping the database out of the model.`,history:`In August 2003 Eric Evans published 'Domain-Driven Design: Tackling Complexity in the Heart of Software', drawn from his years of consulting on business systems. In 2005 Martin Fowler described event sourcing, storing every change to a model as an event instead of only its latest state. Around 2010 Greg Young popularised CQRS, which separates the model that changes data from the one that answers questions. In 2013 Vaughn Vernon's 'Implementing Domain-Driven Design' gave practical guidance, and Alberto Brandolini began EventStorming workshops, in which experts map a business on a wall of sticky notes. In 2014 the first writings on microservices recommended drawing service borders along bounded contexts.`,facts:[`Evans's book, nicknamed the blue book, ran to over 500 pages (2003).`,`Vernon's red book followed ten years later (2013).`]},"tech:imgui":{intro:`An immediate-mode GUI throws away the usual tree of button and window objects. Instead, the program simply says 'draw a button here' every time it draws a frame, and in the same breath asks whether that button was just clicked. There is no copy of the interface to keep in step with the program's data, so tools and debug screens take a few lines.`,how:`A call such as Button('Save') both draws the button and returns true on the frame it was clicked, so the code that handles it sits right beside the code that shows it. The library keeps only a little state, such as which widget the mouse is over, identified by its label or position. Because the whole interface is rebuilt every frame, it can never be out of date. It suits games and tools that redraw constantly anyway, and suits polished, accessible consumer apps less well.`,history:`In 2002 Casey Muratori used the technique in tools for Granny, a 3D animation library sold by RAD Game Tools. In 2005 Muratori published a video lecture that named the approach immediate-mode GUI and explained it to other game programmers. In 2014 Omar Cornut released Dear ImGui as open source in C++, after first using it at Media Molecule on the game Tearaway. In January 2019 the egui library brought the idea to the Rust language.`,facts:[`Dear ImGui had about 76,800 stars on GitHub (October 2026).`,`Dear ImGui's core is a handful of C++ files with no outside dependencies (2014).`]},"tech:xaml":{intro:`Declarative desktop UI describes a program's windows in a markup file, much like a web page, instead of building them in code. Each field on the screen is bound to a piece of data, so when the data changes the screen follows, and designers can restyle the whole application without touching its logic.`,how:`In WPF a window is written in XAML, an XML language in which a Grid holds a TextBox and a Button. A binding such as {Binding CustomerName} ties the text box to a property of a view-model, a plain class holding the screen's state and commands; the pattern is called MVVM, model-view-viewmodel. Styles and templates can redraw any control completely. Everything is drawn through DirectX as vector graphics, so windows stay sharp at any screen resolution.`,history:`In October 2003 Microsoft showed Avalon, a new graphics and interface system, at its developer conference as part of its plans for Windows Longhorn. In 2004 Macromedia's Flex used a similar markup, MXML, for rich applications running in Flash. In October 2005 John Gossman, an architect of Microsoft's design tools, described the MVVM pattern on his blog. On 6 November 2006 WPF shipped as part of .NET Framework 3.0, for Windows XP and the coming Windows Vista. In September 2007 Microsoft released Silverlight, a cut-down XAML system for web browsers. In 2008 NBC used Silverlight to stream the Beijing Olympics online.`,facts:[`Qt Quick and its QML markup brought the same idea to Qt in 2010.`,`Microsoft made WPF open source in December 2018.`]},"tech:funcmain":{intro:`Functional ideas go mainstream when everyday languages such as C#, Java and JavaScript borrow the best tricks of the functional ones. Programmers pass small functions around as values, write 'filter the orders, map them to totals, add them up' instead of a hand-made loop, and treat more of their data as unchangeable.`,how:`A lambda is a short, nameless function written inline, such as x => x * 2, and a closure is a lambda that remembers variables from where it was made. Collection methods such as map, filter and reduce take lambdas and do the looping themselves, so code says what is wanted instead of how. Immutable data and pure functions can be shared between processor cores without locks, which matters as chips gain cores instead of speed. Option types replace many null checks.`,history:`In 2004 Martin Odersky at the EPFL in Lausanne released Scala, mixing functional and object-oriented programming on Java's runtime. In 2004 Google's MapReduce paper borrowed two functional ideas, map and reduce, to process data across thousands of machines. In March 2005 Herb Sutter's article 'The Free Lunch Is Over' warned that chips would gain cores, not speed, so programs had to become concurrent. In 2007 Rich Hickey released Clojure, a Lisp for the Java platform, and in November 2007 C# 3.0 added lambdas and LINQ queries to Microsoft's main language. In December 2009 ECMAScript 5 gave every JavaScript array map, filter and reduce. In March 2014 Java 8 added lambdas and streams, bringing the style to millions of programmers.`,facts:[`Microsoft Research's F# brought ML-style programming to .NET in 2005.`,`Java 1.0 (1996) waited 18 years for lambdas, which came with Java 8 (2014).`]},"tech:cleancode":{intro:`Clean Code and SOLID are a popular set of rules for writing object-oriented code that stays readable and easy to change. Functions should be short and do one thing, names should explain themselves, and each class should have a single reason to change. The rules are much loved, much argued about and rarely measured.`,how:`SOLID stands for five principles. Single responsibility: a class does one job. Open-closed: extend behaviour by adding code, not by editing old code. Liskov substitution: a subclass must work wherever its parent does. Interface segregation: many small interfaces beat one large one. Dependency inversion: depend on abstract interfaces, not concrete classes. Clean Code adds habits such as tiny functions, few arguments and tests for everything. Critics answer that the many small layers make code slower and harder to follow.`,history:`In 1987 Barbara Liskov described the substitution rule that bears her name in a keynote on data abstraction. In 1988 Bertrand Meyer stated the open-closed principle in his book 'Object-Oriented Software Construction'. In 2000 Robert Martin, known as Uncle Bob, gathered five principles in his paper 'Design Principles and Design Patterns'. In February 2001 Martin was among the 17 authors of the Agile Manifesto, written at a ski resort in Utah. In August 2008 his book 'Clean Code' became a standard text for new programmers. In February 2023 Casey Muratori's video 'Clean Code, Horrible Performance' showed the same calculation running up to about 15 times faster when the rules were broken.`,facts:[`Michael Feathers noticed around 2004 that the five initials spelled SOLID.`,`Clean Code said functions should hardly ever be 20 lines long (2008).`]},"tech:reactive":{intro:`Reactive programming treats events, such as mouse clicks, network messages, stock prices or sensor readings, as streams of values flowing over time. Instead of writing a separate callback for each event, the programmer builds the program by joining, filtering and transforming whole streams, the way a spreadsheet recalculates when a cell changes.`,how:`In Rx a stream is an observable, and a subscriber receives its values one by one, followed by an end or an error. Operators work on streams as functional methods work on lists: map transforms each value, filter drops some, merge combines two streams, and throttle or debounce slow a fast stream down. A search box becomes 'keystrokes, debounced by 300 milliseconds, mapped to server requests, keeping only the latest answer'. Errors and cancellation travel through the same pipes.`,history:`In 1979 VisiCalc showed business users the reactive idea: change one cell and every formula that uses it updates. In 1994 the Gang of Four's book described the Observer pattern, in which objects subscribe to changes in another. In 1997 Conal Elliott and Paul Hudak described functional reactive programming in their paper 'Functional Reactive Animation'. In 2009 Erik Meijer's team at Microsoft released Reactive Extensions, Rx, for .NET. In 2013 Netflix released RxJava, a port of the by then open-source Rx, to handle the many parallel calls behind its service. In September 2016 Angular 2 made the RxJS library a core part of the framework.`,facts:[`The second version of the Reactive Manifesto appeared on 16 September 2014.`,`Angular 16 adopted signals, a simpler reactive model (May 2023).`]},"tech:observability":{intro:`Monitoring and distributed tracing let engineers see inside a large system while it runs. Logs record what each program did, metrics count things such as requests and errors every few seconds, and a trace follows one user's request through every service it touches, showing exactly where it slowed down or failed.`,how:`Each service writes logs and exports metrics to a central store, where dashboards plot them and alerts wake an engineer when a number crosses a line. For tracing, the first service gives each request a unique trace ID and passes it along with every call it makes; each service records a span, a timed piece of work tagged with that ID. Joined together, the spans show the request's whole journey as a timeline. Because tracing everything is costly, systems keep only a sample.`,history:`In 1960 the engineer Rudolf Kálmán defined observability in control theory: how well a system's inner state can be known from its outputs. In the 1980s Eric Allman's syslog, written alongside Sendmail, gave Unix programs one common way to write logs. In 1999 Ethan Galstad released NetSaint, renamed Nagios, which checked servers and paged engineers when one failed. In April 2010 Google published a paper on Dapper, the tracing system running across its production services. In June 2012 Twitter released Zipkin, an open-source tracer modelled on Dapper, and SoundCloud began the Prometheus metrics system that year. In May 2019 the OpenTracing and OpenCensus projects merged into OpenTelemetry, one standard for traces, metrics and logs.`,facts:[`Dapper traced only about one request in every 1,024 to keep its cost low (2010).`,`Datadog, founded in 2010, went public in September 2019.`]},"tech:fuzzing":{intro:`Fuzzing finds bugs by feeding a program huge numbers of random or mangled inputs and waiting for it to crash. Modern fuzzers are clever about it: they watch which parts of the code each input reaches and breed new inputs from those that explore somewhere new, running millions of tests a day without anyone writing a single one.`,how:`A coverage-guided fuzzer such as AFL compiles the target with extra instructions that record which branches each run takes. It starts from a few sample files, then mutates them: flipping bits, inserting bytes, splicing two files together. Any input that reaches a new branch is kept and mutated further, so the fuzzer slowly learns the file format by trial and error. Paired with memory checkers, which turn silent corruption into an instant crash, it finds bugs no human tester would think to try.`,history:`In 1988 Barton Miller at the University of Wisconsin noticed that noise on a dial-up line crashed Unix programs, and set his students to test utilities with random input. In 1990 his paper showed how many standard Unix tools failed. In 2008 Microsoft researchers described SAGE, a fuzzer that solved equations to steer its inputs down untested paths. In November 2013 Michał Zalewski released American Fuzzy Lop, AFL, whose coverage-guided approach soon found bugs in nearly every major open-source library. In April 2014 the Heartbleed flaw in OpenSSL, found independently by a Google engineer and by Codenomicon's testing tools, showed how much of the internet relied on poorly tested code. In December 2016 Google launched OSS-Fuzz, offering free fuzzing on its servers to important open-source projects.`,facts:[`Random input crashed or hung 25 to 33% of the Unix utilities tested (1990).`,`By May 2025 OSS-Fuzz had helped fix over 13,000 vulnerabilities and 50,000 bugs in 1,000 projects.`]},"tech:permissive":{intro:`A permissive licence lets anyone use, change and redistribute a program's source code, even inside closed, commercial products, asking little more than that the original copyright notice be kept and that the authors not be sued. Unlike copyleft licences, it does not require anyone who builds on the code to share their own changes.`,how:`The BSD and MIT licences are a few short paragraphs. They grant permission to use, copy, modify and sell the software; require the copyright notice and licence text to travel with every copy; and disclaim any warranty, so the authors are not liable if it fails. The original BSD licence also asked that advertisements credit the University of California. Because the terms are so light, companies adopt such code without legal worries, which spreads it fast; but rivals owe nothing back.`,history:`In 1987 MIT released X11 under a short permissive licence of its own. In 1988 the University of California, Berkeley began releasing code from its BSD Unix under a brief licence that allowed almost any use. In 1991 Berkeley's Net/2 tape put nearly all of BSD Unix under that licence, opening the way to free versions of the system. In 1994 a settlement of the Unix lawsuit brought by AT&T's Unix System Laboratories left almost all of the BSD code free to use. On 22 July 1999 Berkeley dropped the clause requiring advertisements to credit the university. In 2001 Apple shipped Mac OS X, built partly on BSD code.`,facts:[`45% of licensed GitHub repositories used the MIT licence (2015).`,`Sony built the PlayStation 4's system software on FreeBSD (2013).`]},"tech:weakcopyleft":{intro:`A library licence is a middle way between giving code away freely and requiring everything built on it to be shared. Changes to the library itself must be published under the same licence, but a closed, commercial program may link to the library and keep its own code private.`,how:`The GNU Lesser General Public License works at the level of the library: a program that merely calls the library can carry any licence, as long as users can swap in a modified version of the library. The Mozilla Public License works file by file: any changed file from the original code must stay open, while new files added beside it can be closed. Both keep improvements to the shared part flowing back, while letting companies build products on top.`,history:`In June 1991 the Free Software Foundation published the GNU Library General Public License alongside version 2 of the GPL, so that the GNU C library could be used by any program. In 1998 Netscape released its browser's source under the Netscape Public License, and the Mozilla Public License, drafted by Mitchell Baker, followed that year. In February 1999 the FSF renamed its licence the Lesser GPL and urged authors to choose the full GPL for libraries where they could. In June 2007 version 3 of the LGPL appeared together with GPLv3. In 2009 Nokia put Qt under the LGPL, so companies could use it free in closed products. In January 2012 version 2.0 of the Mozilla licence was made compatible with the GPL.`,facts:[`Netscape released its browser source code on 31 March 1998.`,`Firefox 1.0, built from MPL-licensed code, arrived in November 2004.`]},"tech:osfoundation":{intro:`An open-source foundation is a non-profit organisation that owns a project's code, name and trademarks on behalf of everyone who works on it. Because no single company controls it, competitors can safely put their engineers on the same code, knowing that none of them can take it private or steer it alone.`,how:`The foundation holds licences from contributors, owns the trademark, pays for servers and gives legal protection. Each project is run by a committee of its most active contributors, chosen for their work and not their employer; Apache calls this meritocracy. Decisions are made in the open on mailing lists, usually by vote. New projects enter through an incubator, where they learn the rules before graduating. In return, no company can relicense the code or own its future.`,history:`In 1995 a group of webmasters began sharing patches to the abandoned NCSA web server, and in April 1996 their Apache server became the most popular on the web. On 25 March 1999 the Apache Group incorporated as the Apache Software Foundation, to protect its members from lawsuits and hold the project's assets. In 1999 Sun gave the foundation the code of its Tomcat Java server. In January 2004 the Apache License 2.0 added an explicit grant of patent rights. In 2004 IBM and its partners turned their Eclipse project into the independent Eclipse Foundation. In 2008 Hadoop became a top-level Apache project.`,facts:[`Apache httpd ran about half of all web servers in 1999 and 67% in March 2004.`,`Richard Stallman had set up the Free Software Foundation in 1985.`]},"tech:duallicense":{intro:`Dual licensing offers the same code under two licences. Anyone may use it free under a copyleft licence such as the GPL, on condition that they share their own program's code too; a company that wants to build it into a closed product buys a commercial licence instead. Free users spread the product, and paying ones fund it.`,how:`The scheme works only if the company owns every line, so outside contributors must sign over their rights, or the company writes nearly everything itself. Most users, such as websites running a database on their own servers, never distribute the program and owe nothing. The paying customers are equipment and software makers who ship the code inside their own products and do not want to open theirs. Support and extra tools are often sold alongside.`,history:`In 1995 Michael Widenius, David Axmark and Allan Larsson released the MySQL database in Sweden under a licence of their own. In 1996 Sleepycat Software was founded to sell Berkeley DB, free for open-source programs and licensed for closed ones. In 2000 MySQL AB moved MySQL to the GPL, selling commercial licences to companies that embedded it, and Trolltech did the same with its Qt toolkit. In 2006 Oracle bought Sleepycat. On 16 January 2008 Sun agreed to buy MySQL AB for about $1 billion. In 2009, as Oracle moved to take over Sun, Widenius forked MySQL as MariaDB.`,facts:[`MySQL AB had about 400 staff when Sun bought it (2008).`,`Oracle bought Sun, and with it MySQL, for about $7.4 billion (2010).`]},"tech:netcopyleft":{intro:`A network copyleft licence closes a gap in the GPL. The GPL requires sharing the source code only when someone hands out copies of a program, but a company running it as a website hands out nothing. The Affero GPL counts the users of a web service too: offer modified code to people over a network, and you must offer them its source.`,how:`The licence is the GPL with one added clause. If you modify the program and let users interact with it remotely, you must give those users a way to download the complete source of your version, for example through a link in the program. Running unmodified code needs nothing extra. Because the duty reaches any company that hosts the code, cautious companies avoid it, and authors use it to stop rivals offering their software as a closed service.`,history:`In March 2002 the start-up Affero, working with the Free Software Foundation, published the Affero General Public License. On 19 November 2007 the FSF published the GNU Affero GPL version 3, a companion to the new GPLv3. In March 2008 the Open Source Initiative approved it as an open-source licence. In 2009 MongoDB released its database server under the AGPL. In October 2018 MongoDB left the AGPL for a stricter licence of its own. In April 2021 Grafana Labs moved its dashboard software from the Apache licence to the AGPL to deter cloud rivals.`,facts:[`The AGPL's key addition is Section 13, on remote network interaction (2007).`,`Mastodon, the federated social network software, was released under the AGPL (2016).`]},"tech:industryfound":{intro:`An industry foundation is a non-profit club that rival companies pay to join, pooling money and engineers to build software they all depend on. Instead of each firm keeping its own team to work on Linux or another shared project, members split the cost, set priorities together and keep the result neutral.`,how:`Members pay yearly dues by tier, with the largest firms buying seats on the board. The foundation employs a small staff for legal work, events, training, certification and marketing, and holds the projects' trademarks and servers. Each hosted project has its own technical committee, kept apart from the business board, so code decisions stay with engineers. Membership fees, conferences and training courses pay the bills.`,history:`In 2000 IBM, HP, Intel and others funded the Open Source Development Labs (OSDL) in Oregon to make Linux ready for business computing. In June 2003 OSDL hired Linus Torvalds, so he could work on the kernel full time while staying neutral between companies. In January 2007 OSDL merged with the Free Standards Group to form the Linux Foundation, led by Jim Zemlin. In 2015 the foundation began hosting other industries' shared projects, from the Node.js Foundation to the Cloud Native Computing Foundation. In 2017 its kernel report showed that companies, not volunteers, wrote most of Linux.`,facts:[`Over 85% of Linux kernel work came from paid developers and 8.2% from volunteers (2017).`,`The Linux Foundation hosted more than 1,000 projects by 2025.`]},"tech:opencore":{intro:`Open core is a business model in which a company gives away the core of its product as open source and sells extra features on top. The free edition attracts a large community of users and contributors; the paid enterprise edition adds what big companies need, such as security controls, management tools, scale and support.`,how:`The line between free and paid is the whole strategy. Features that individual developers need go into the open core, so it spreads; features that only large organisations need, such as single sign-on, audit logs, clustering and dashboards, go into the closed edition. Usually only a small share of users ever pays. Drawn too tight, the line angers the community, which may call the free edition crippleware or start a fork.`,history:`In 2001 Martin Roesch founded Sourcefire to sell commercial products around Snort, the free intrusion detector he had written. In 2004 SugarCRM released a free community edition of its sales software beside paid editions. In 2008 Andrew Lampitt coined the term open core for this mix. In 2009 JetBrains released IntelliJ IDEA Community Edition as open source, keeping its Ultimate edition paid. In 2013 Cisco bought Sourcefire. In October 2021 GitLab, which sold paid editions of its open-source code platform, listed its shares on the Nasdaq.`,facts:[`Cisco paid about $2.7 billion for Sourcefire (2013).`,`Elastic, another open-core company, went public in October 2018.`]},"tech:sourceavail":{intro:`A source-available licence lets anyone read and run a program's code, but forbids certain uses, above all offering it to others as a paid cloud service. Companies that once released their software as open source switch to such licences when cloud providers start renting it out without paying them anything.`,how:`There are several designs. The Server Side Public License requires anyone offering the software as a service to publish the source of their entire service stack, which no cloud company will do. The Business Source License forbids some production uses, but each version turns into a true open-source licence at a set change date, at most four years later. Because these licences treat some users differently, they fail the Open Source Definition, and many Linux distributions drop such software.`,history:`In 2016 MariaDB released its MaxScale database proxy under the Business Source License. In October 2018 MongoDB switched its database to its new Server Side Public License. In January 2019 Red Hat and Debian removed MongoDB from their distributions. In January 2021 Elastic moved Elasticsearch to source-available licences, and in April 2021 Amazon launched OpenSearch, a fork of the last open version. In August 2023 HashiCorp moved Terraform to the Business Source License, and in September 2023 the community fork OpenTofu joined the Linux Foundation. In March 2024 Redis changed its licence and the Linux Foundation launched the Valkey fork, while in August 2024 Elastic added the open-source AGPL back as an option.`,facts:[`OpenTofu forked Terraform within weeks of its licence change (2023).`,`Redis 8 added the AGPL as an option in May 2025.`]},"tech:logsearch":{intro:`Machine-data search collects the log files that every server, program and network device writes, and makes them searchable like the web. Instead of logging into dozens of machines to hunt through text files, an engineer types a query and sees every matching event from the whole data centre, in time order, within seconds.`,how:`Collectors on each machine forward log lines to a central indexer, which stamps each line with its time and source and builds an index of every word, so any term can be found at once. Unlike a database, it needs no fixed format in advance: fields such as user, status or response time are pulled out at search time, an approach called schema on read. A pipeline query language counts, sorts and charts the results, and saved searches drive dashboards and alerts.`,history:`In 2003 Michael Baum, Rob Das and Erik Swan founded Splunk in San Francisco, naming it after spelunking, the exploring of caves. In late 2005 Splunk shipped version 1.0 of its search software after a public beta. In February 2010 Shay Banon released Elasticsearch, which with the Logstash collector and the Kibana dashboard formed the free ELK stack. In 2010 Datadog was founded in New York to monitor servers in the cloud. In April 2012 Splunk went public on the Nasdaq. In March 2024 Cisco completed its purchase of Splunk, the largest acquisition in its history.`,facts:[`Cisco paid about $28 billion for Splunk (2024).`,`Splunk's shares roughly doubled on their first day of trading (2012).`]},"tech:hadoop":{intro:`Hadoop is free software that lets any company store and process enormous amounts of data on hundreds or thousands of cheap servers, the way Google does. It splits files into big blocks spread across the machines and runs programs where the data already sits, so a cluster can chew through huge data sets in hours.`,how:`The Hadoop Distributed File System cuts each file into large blocks and keeps three copies of every block on different machines, so a failed disk loses nothing. One master server, the NameNode, tracks where every block lives. MapReduce jobs run in two phases: map tasks on each machine read local blocks and emit key-value pairs, which are sorted and sent to reduce tasks that combine them, for example counting every word on the web. Failed tasks are simply rerun elsewhere.`,history:`In 2003 and 2004 Google published papers on its GFS file system and its MapReduce programming model. In 2004 and 2005 Doug Cutting and Mike Cafarella rebuilt both ideas in Java inside Nutch, their open-source web search engine. In January 2006 Cutting joined Yahoo, and the storage and processing code was split out of Nutch as Hadoop, named after his son's yellow toy elephant. In February 2008 Yahoo's search index, the Webmap, ran on Hadoop across more than 10,000 processor cores. In July 2008 a Yahoo Hadoop cluster won the annual terabyte sort benchmark. In 2009 Cutting joined Cloudera, a start-up founded the year before to sell Hadoop to businesses.`,facts:[`Hadoop sorted a terabyte in 209 seconds on 910 machines (2008).`,`Facebook's Hadoop cluster held 21 PB in 2010 and 100 PB in 2012.`]},"tech:sqlonhadoop":{intro:`SQL on big data lets analysts query a Hadoop cluster in SQL, the language they already know from databases, instead of writing MapReduce programs in Java. A question such as 'total sales by country last year' is translated into jobs that run across thousands of machines, over data far too large for any ordinary database.`,how:`Hive keeps a metastore, a catalogue that describes files in the cluster as tables with columns and types, without changing the files themselves. Its compiler turns a HiveQL query into a chain of MapReduce jobs. Because each query starts batch jobs, answers take minutes instead of seconds, but they can cover petabytes. Tables can be partitioned by date, so a query about one week reads only that week's files.`,history:`In 2007 Facebook engineers Joydeep Sen Sarma and Ashish Thusoo began Hive so that analysts could use the company's fast-growing Hadoop cluster. In 2008 Facebook released Hive as open source, and Yahoo's Pig, a data-flow language for the same job, reached its first release. In 2010 Hive became a top-level Apache project, and Google described Dremel, which answered SQL queries over huge tables in seconds. In October 2012 Cloudera announced Impala, a fast SQL engine for Hadoop built on Dremel's ideas. In November 2013 Facebook released Presto, which queried data where it lay in seconds instead of minutes. In December 2020 Presto's original creators renamed their branch of it Trino.`,facts:[`Google's Dremel ran aggregate queries over trillion-row tables in seconds (2010).`,`Spark SQL brought SQL to the faster Spark engine in 2014.`]},"tech:datascience":{intro:`Data science is a new job and a new discipline, half statistician and half programmer. Data scientists dig through a company's records of clicks, purchases and messages to find patterns, build models that predict what users will do, and turn the results into products, such as recommendations, or into decisions.`,how:`The work runs from question to product. A data scientist gathers and cleans raw data, often the slowest step; explores it with charts and statistics; builds models, from simple regressions to machine-learning classifiers; checks them on data they have not seen; and explains the findings to managers in plain words. Tools range from SQL and Hadoop for gathering to Python, R and notebooks for analysis. Experiments such as A/B tests show whether a finding really changes behaviour.`,history:`In 1962 the statistician John Tukey's paper 'The Future of Data Analysis' argued that analysing data was a science of its own. In October 2006 Netflix offered a $1 million prize to anyone who could improve its film recommendations by 10%. In 2008 DJ Patil at LinkedIn and Jeff Hammerbacher at Facebook began calling their analysts data scientists. In May 2011 the McKinsey Global Institute warned of a large shortage of people with deep analytical skills. In October 2012 the Harvard Business Review called data scientist 'the sexiest job of the 21st century'. In February 2015 Patil became the first chief data scientist of the United States.`,facts:[`The Netflix Prize was won with a 10.06% improvement (2009).`,`McKinsey forecast a US shortage of up to 190,000 deep analytical experts (2011).`]},"tech:datalake":{intro:`A data lake is one huge, cheap store into which a company pours all its raw data, such as logs, clicks, sensor readings, documents and database exports, in its original form and without tidying it first. The questions are decided later: analysts and programs read what they need and give it structure only when they use it.`,how:`A traditional warehouse cleans data and fits it into designed tables before loading, called schema on write, which is costly and throws detail away. A lake keeps everything as files on cheap distributed storage, such as a Hadoop cluster, and applies structure at read time with tools like Hive or Spark. A catalogue records what each data set is, where it came from and who may see it. Without that care the lake turns into a swamp of files nobody understands or trusts.`,history:`In 1992 Bill Inmon's book 'Building the Data Warehouse' set out how to gather a company's data into carefully modelled stores for business reports. In March 2006 Amazon launched S3, storage rented by the gigabyte, which made keeping raw data cheap. In October 2010 James Dixon, technical chief of the analytics firm Pentaho, coined the term data lake, contrasting it with the neat bottled water of a data mart. In 2013 Twitter and Cloudera created the Parquet columnar file format, which made large files in a lake fast to query. In July 2014 the research firm Gartner warned of a 'data lake fallacy', in which lakes became dumping grounds. In April 2019 Databricks released Delta Lake as open source, adding database-style transactions to the files in a lake.`,facts:[`IDC counted 1.2 zettabytes of data created in 2010 and 64.2 zettabytes in 2020.`,`Amazon S3 launched at 15 cents per gigabyte per month (2006).`]},"tech:streaming":{intro:`Event streaming publishes every event in a company, such as a click, a payment or a sensor reading, into one durable log that any system can read as it happens. Instead of copying data between databases every night, each application writes its events once, and every other system subscribes to the streams it needs.`,how:`Kafka stores each stream, called a topic, as an append-only log split into partitions spread over many servers and copied to several of them for safety. Producers add messages at the end; consumers read at their own pace and remember their position, called an offset, so a slow or new consumer can catch up or replay history. Messages are kept for days or for ever, not deleted when read. Because it writes to disk in sequence, it can handle millions of messages a second.`,history:`In 1993 IBM's MQSeries carried messages between business programs, each one deleted once it was delivered. In 2010 Jay Kreps, Neha Narkhede and Jun Rao at LinkedIn began Kafka, named after the writer Franz Kafka, to move the site's activity data in real time. Early in 2011 LinkedIn released Kafka as open source, and in July 2011 it entered the Apache incubator. On 23 October 2012 Kafka became a top-level Apache project. In December 2013 Kreps's essay 'The Log' argued that an ordered log of events could be the backbone of a company's data. In 2014 the three founded Confluent to sell and support Kafka.`,facts:[`LinkedIn's Kafka passed 1 trillion messages a day (2015).`,`Confluent's revenue reached $964 million (2024).`]},"tech:vectordb":{intro:`A vector database stores items as lists of numbers, called embeddings, that capture their meaning, and finds the items whose numbers are closest to those of a query. Two sentences that mean the same thing get nearby vectors even if they share no words, so 'find similar' becomes a geometry problem the database solves in milliseconds.`,how:`A machine-learning model turns each document, picture or product into a vector of hundreds or thousands of numbers. Comparing a query with every stored vector would be far too slow at scale, so the database builds an approximate nearest-neighbour index, such as HNSW, a layered graph that hops quickly towards the right neighbourhood, or clusters that are searched only near the query. It trades a tiny loss of accuracy for enormous speed, and filters results by ordinary fields such as date or owner.`,history:`In 2013 Tomas Mikolov's team at Google released word2vec, which turned words into vectors whose distances reflected meaning. In March 2017 Facebook released FAISS, a library for similarity search over billions of vectors. In January 2020 Milvus, a vector database from the Shanghai company Zilliz, joined the Linux Foundation's AI foundation. In 2020 Facebook researchers described retrieval-augmented generation, in which a language model looks up relevant passages before it answers. In 2021 Pinecone launched a hosted vector database, and pgvector added vector search to PostgreSQL. In 2023, after ChatGPT's launch, nearly every major database added vector search.`,facts:[`OpenAI's ada-002 embeddings described each text with 1,536 numbers (2022).`,`Pinecone raised $100 million at a $750 million valuation (2023).`]},"tech:chroot":{intro:`Changed root, or chroot, tells a program that one folder is the top of the whole file system. Everything outside that folder simply disappears from the program's view, so a server, a test build or a suspicious program can be shut inside a small corner of the disk with only the files it needs.`,how:`A Unix process normally finds files starting from the root directory, written /. The chroot system call, which only the superuser may make, moves that starting point to another directory for the process and all its children. Paths beginning with / now begin there, and '..' at the new top leads nowhere. Any programs and libraries the confined process needs must be copied inside. It isolates files only: processes, users and the network are still shared, and a process running as root can find ways out.`,history:`In 1979, during the development of Version 7 Unix at Bell Labs, the chroot call was added to the system. On 18 March 1982 Bill Joy added chroot to Berkeley's BSD Unix, to test builds of the system in a clean directory. In 1991 Bill Cheswick of AT&T Bell Labs trapped an intruder he nicknamed Berferd inside a chroot 'jail' built to look like a real system, and watched what he did. By the mid-1990s anonymous FTP servers commonly used chroot so that visitors saw only the public files. In 2000 FreeBSD built its jails on the idea, sealing off processes and networking as well.`,facts:[`Version 7 Unix, where chroot first appeared, was released in 1979.`,`Cheswick's 1992 paper 'An Evening with Berferd' made 'jail' the word for such a prison.`]},"tech:jails":{intro:`OS-level virtualisation splits one computer into many walled gardens that all share the same operating system kernel. Each garden has its own files, users, processes and network address, and the programs inside believe they have the machine to themselves; with no separate operating system to boot, hundreds can run on one server.`,how:`The kernel tags every process with the jail or zone it belongs to and checks the tag on every request. A process sees only processes of its own jail, only its own part of the file system and only its own network addresses, and even its root user cannot touch the host or load kernel code. Because there is one shared kernel and no emulated hardware, a jail starts in a moment and uses almost no extra memory, but every jail must run the same operating system.`,history:`On 14 March 2000 FreeBSD 4.0 introduced jails, written by Poul-Henning Kamp for a web-hosting company that wanted to give customers root access safely. In 2000 Kamp and Robert Watson described the design in the paper 'Jails: Confining the Omnipotent Root'. By 2001 SWsoft's Virtuozzo was selling the same idea for Linux to hosting firms. In February 2004 Sun previewed Solaris Zones in a test version of Solaris 10. In 2005 Solaris 10 shipped with Zones, and SWsoft released OpenVZ, a free version of Virtuozzo. In January 2008 Linux 2.6.24 added cgroups, which limit how much processor time and memory a group of processes may use, and LXC combined them with namespaces into containers that year.`,facts:[`Linux's first namespace, for mount points, arrived in 2002.`,`cgroups were written by Google engineers Paul Menage and Rohit Seth (2007).`]},"tech:iac":{intro:`Infrastructure as code means describing servers in text files instead of setting each one up by hand. A file says which packages a web server needs, which users exist and what its configuration contains; a tool reads the file and makes every matching machine look exactly like that, again and again, across thousands of servers.`,how:`In Puppet the administrator writes manifests that declare the desired state, such as 'package nginx is installed, service nginx is running', and an agent on each server checks the real state every half hour and corrects any difference. Running the same manifest twice changes nothing the second time, a property called idempotence. Because manifests live in version control, changes are reviewed, tested and rolled back like program code, and a failed server can be rebuilt in minutes.`,history:`In 1993 Mark Burgess at Oslo University College wrote CFEngine to keep the college's Unix machines configured. In 2005 Luke Kanies released Puppet, with a friendlier language for describing systems. In 2009 Adam Jacob's company Opscode released Chef, written in Ruby. In February 2011 Amazon launched CloudFormation, which described whole cloud setups in templates. In 2012 Michael DeHaan released Ansible, which needed no agent on the servers. In July 2014 HashiCorp released Terraform, which created cloud resources across many providers from one description.`,facts:[`Progress Software bought Chef for $220 million (2020).`,`IBM paid $6.4 billion for HashiCorp, maker of Terraform (2025).`]},"tech:paas":{intro:`Platform as a service lets developers hand over just their program while the provider supplies and runs everything underneath it. There are no servers to buy, install, patch or watch: push the code, and the platform builds it, starts it on its machines, connects a database and adds more copies when traffic grows.`,how:`The developer pushes code, often with one git command. The platform detects the language, fetches libraries, builds a runnable package and starts it in isolated containers on its own servers, behind a router that spreads requests among them. Databases, caches and email come as add-on services. The platform restarts crashed copies and scales with a slider or automatically. The price is control: only supported languages run, apps must follow the platform's rules, and the bill grows with success.`,history:`In March 2006 Zimki, from the London company Fotango, opened a beta of hosted JavaScript applications billed by use. In June 2007 James Lindenbaum, Adam Wiggins and Orion Henry started Heroku to host Ruby on Rails apps. On 7 April 2008 Google previewed App Engine, which ran Python web apps on Google's own infrastructure, free up to a quota. In October 2008 Microsoft announced Azure, at first mainly a platform of this kind. In December 2010 Salesforce bought Heroku for $212 million. In April 2011 VMware launched Cloud Foundry, and in May 2011 Red Hat launched OpenShift, platforms that companies could also run on their own servers.`,facts:[`App Engine's free quota covered about 5 million page views a month (2008).`,`Adam Wiggins published the Twelve-Factor App rules for building such apps (2011).`]},"tech:cncf":{intro:`The Cloud Native Computing Foundation is a neutral home for the software that runs modern cloud applications, starting with Kubernetes. By giving its container system to a foundation, Google let rival clouds and vendors adopt and improve it without fearing that one company would own the industry's plumbing.`,how:`The foundation, part of the Linux Foundation, owns the trademarks and hosts projects at three levels, sandbox, incubating and graduated, according to how mature and widely used they are. Members pay dues that fund conferences such as KubeCon, security audits, documentation and testing. A certification programme checks that each company's Kubernetes product behaves the same, so applications can move between clouds. Technical decisions stay with each project's maintainers.`,history:`Around 2004 Google began running its services on Borg, an internal system that packed jobs onto huge clusters of machines. In June 2014 Google announced Kubernetes, an open-source system drawing on Borg's lessons, started by Joe Beda, Brendan Burns and Craig McLuckie. On 21 July 2015 Kubernetes reached version 1.0, and Google gave it to the newly formed CNCF. In May 2016 Prometheus became the foundation's second project. In late 2017 Docker added Kubernetes support and Amazon announced its own managed Kubernetes service, ending the contest between orchestrators. In March 2018 Kubernetes became the first project to graduate from the foundation.`,facts:[`Containers in production rose from 23% of CNCF survey respondents (2016) to 84% (2019).`,`Kubernetes 1.0 shipped on 21 July 2015, about a year after its announcement.`]},"tech:servicemesh":{intro:`A service mesh is a layer of tiny network proxies placed beside every service in a microservice system. The proxies handle all the talking between services: they encrypt traffic, retry failed calls, balance load, enforce who may call whom and record every request, so the services' own code does not have to.`,how:`Each service instance gets a sidecar proxy beside it, and all its traffic flows through that proxy. Together the proxies form the data plane. A control plane sends them configuration: routing rules, certificates for mutual TLS so each service proves its identity, timeouts and retry limits, and rules such as sending 5% of traffic to a new version. Because the proxies see every request, they also report detailed metrics and traces. The cost is an extra hop, and a little latency, on every call.`,history:`In 2011 Twitter open-sourced Finagle, a library that handled retries and load balancing inside each service. In February 2016 Buoyant, founded by former Twitter engineers William Morgan and Oliver Gould, released Linkerd and soon coined the term service mesh. In September 2016 Lyft released Envoy, the proxy written by Matt Klein, as open source. In January 2017 Linkerd joined the CNCF, and in May 2017 Google, IBM and Lyft announced Istio, built on Envoy. In July 2021 Linkerd became the first service mesh to graduate from the CNCF. In July 2023 Istio graduated too.`,facts:[`Envoy joined the CNCF in September 2017.`,`Istio 1.0 was released in July 2018.`]},"tech:microvm":{intro:`A microVM is a stripped-down virtual machine that starts in a fraction of a second and uses only a few megabytes of memory. It gives each customer's code the strong walls of a real virtual machine with nearly the speed and density of a container, so a cloud can safely run thousands of strangers' functions side by side on one server.`,how:`Firecracker is a virtual machine monitor that uses the processor's built-in virtualisation through Linux's KVM. It emulates almost nothing: a network card, a disk, a serial console and a minimal keyboard controller used only to stop the guest, with no graphics, USB or old PC devices. Less emulated hardware means less code for attackers to probe, and a small Linux guest boots almost at once. Written in the memory-safe language Rust, it runs each microVM as a sandboxed process with tight limits.`,history:`In November 2014 Amazon launched Lambda, which ran customers' code in response to events with no servers to manage. In December 2017 the Kata Containers project merged Intel's Clear Containers with Hyper's runV to put containers inside light virtual machines. In May 2018 Google released gVisor, which shielded containers with a kernel rewritten in Go that runs as an ordinary program. In November 2018 Amazon released Firecracker as open source, built from crosvm, the virtual machine monitor of Google's Chrome OS. By its release in 2018 Firecracker already ran Lambda functions and Fargate containers in production.`,facts:[`A Firecracker microVM booted in about 125 milliseconds with under 5MB of memory overhead (2018).`,`One host could start up to 150 microVMs a second (2018).`]},"tech:devplatform":{intro:`An internal developer platform is a company's own self-service layer over its infrastructure. A dedicated platform team builds paved roads, ready templates and tools for creating, building, deploying and running a service, so product engineers can launch something new in minutes without becoming experts in Kubernetes, cloud accounts and security rules.`,how:`A developer portal lists every service, who owns it, its documentation and its health. Templates create a new service with a code repository, build pipeline, monitoring and security settings already wired up. Behind the portal, the platform team turns containers, infrastructure code and cloud services into a few simple choices. Product teams may leave the road when they must, but the road is easier. The platform team treats other engineers as its customers, measuring adoption and how fast changes reach production.`,history:`In 2016 Spotify began building an internal portal, System Z, to keep track of its fast-growing number of services. In 2019 Matthew Skelton and Manuel Pais's book 'Team Topologies' described platform teams as one of four basic kinds of team. On 16 March 2020 Spotify released its portal as open source under the name Backstage, and in September 2020 it joined the CNCF. In 2022 Gartner named platform engineering one of the top technology trends, predicting that most large software organisations would set up platform teams. In 2022 the first PlatformCon conference gathered platform engineers online.`,facts:[`Gartner predicted that 80% of large software engineering organisations would have platform teams (2022).`,`Backstage moved from the CNCF sandbox to incubation in March 2022.`]},"tech:rtos":{intro:`A real-time operating system promises not just to do a job, but to do it in time. An ordinary system gets round to each task when it can; a real-time one guarantees that the most urgent task, such as reading a sensor, opening a valve or stopping a machine, starts within a fixed number of microseconds, every time.`,how:`Each task gets a priority, and the scheduler always runs the highest-priority task that is ready, interrupting a lower one at once when an urgent event arrives. Interrupt handling, task switching and every system call take a bounded, known time. Tasks coordinate through semaphores, message queues and timers. Many such systems are small enough to sit in read-only memory, and they leave out features that would make timing unpredictable, such as virtual memory and disk caching.`,history:`In July 1969 the executive program of the Apollo Guidance Computer shed low-priority jobs when it was overloaded during the Moon landing, raising its famous 1202 alarms while keeping the essential work running. In 1973 Digital Equipment released RSX-11M, a real-time system for PDP-11 minicomputers led by Dave Cutler. In 1976 Intel's iRMX 80 brought a real-time executive to its microprocessor boards. In 1981 Hunter & Ready launched VRTX, a small real-time kernel for many processors. In 1982 Quantum Software Systems of Ottawa released QNX, a microkernel real-time system for the IBM PC. In 1987 Wind River Systems released VxWorks.`,facts:[`The Apollo Guidance Computer had 2,048 words of erasable memory (1969).`,`VxWorks ran the Mars Pathfinder lander (1997).`]},"tech:ecu":{intro:`An engine control computer is a small microprocessor box that runs a car's engine. Many times a second it reads sensors for engine speed, air flow, temperature and the oxygen left in the exhaust, then decides exactly when each spark plug fires and how much fuel each injector sprays, far more precisely than springs and weights can.`,how:`The heart is a microcontroller with its program and calibration tables burned into read-only memory. For every combination of engine speed and load the tables hold the best spark timing and fuel quantity; the program looks them up, corrects for temperature and for the oxygen sensor's reading, and drives the coils and injectors through power transistors. The oxygen sensor closes the loop, keeping the mixture right for a catalytic converter. Changing the tables changes the engine's character, which is how tuners raise its power.`,history:`In 1967 Bosch's D-Jetronic on the Volkswagen 1600 used analogue electronics to control fuel injection. In 1970 the US Clean Air Act set limits on car exhaust that mechanical controls would struggle to meet. In 1976 Bosch's oxygen sensor went into production on Volvo cars, letting electronics hold the fuel mixture exact. In 1977 GM's MISAR system on the Oldsmobile Toronado made it the first production car with a microprocessor. In 1979 Bosch's Motronic on the BMW 732i controlled both ignition and fuel with one digital computer. In 1981 GM fitted its Computer Command Control to nearly all its cars to meet tighter exhaust rules.`,facts:[`GM's first microprocessor system, MISAR, controlled only the spark timing (1977).`,`Delco Electronics built 28,000 engine computers a day (1988).`]},"tech:canbus":{intro:`The Controller Area Network, or CAN, is a shared data bus that lets all of a car's computers talk over one pair of twisted wires. Any controller can send a message at any time; if two start at once, the more important message wins without either being garbled, and every other controller hears it and keeps what it needs.`,how:`Every message starts with an identifier that also sets its priority. The wires carry a dominant 0 that overrides a recessive 1, so as two controllers send their identifiers bit by bit, the one that sends a 1 and hears a 0 knows it has lost and stops; the lower identifier always wins. A frame holds up to 8 bytes of data and a checksum, every receiver confirms it, and a controller that keeps making errors switches itself off the bus. Speeds reach 1 megabit per second.`,history:`In 1983 Bosch engineers led by Uwe Kiencke began designing a network for cars. In February 1986 Bosch presented CAN at the Society of Automotive Engineers congress in Detroit. In 1987 Intel and Philips shipped the first CAN controller chips. In 1991 Bosch published CAN 2.0, and the Mercedes-Benz S-Class became the first production car to use it. In 1993 CAN became the international standard ISO 11898. In 2015 the researchers Charlie Miller and Chris Valasek took control of a Jeep's brakes and steering from afar through its infotainment system and network, and Fiat Chrysler recalled 1.4 million vehicles.`,facts:[`A CAN frame carries at most 8 bytes of data (1986).`,`From the 2008 model year every new car sold in the US had to use CAN for diagnostics.`]},"tech:rtsched":{intro:`Real-time scheduling theory is the mathematics of deadlines. Given a set of tasks, how often each runs and how long each takes, it proves before a system is ever switched on whether every task will always finish in time, and it shows how a slow, unimportant task holding a shared lock can starve an urgent one.`,how:`Rate-monotonic scheduling gives the highest priority to the task that runs most often. Liu and Layland proved that tasks scheduled this way always meet their deadlines if the processor is busy less than about 69% of the time, and exact tests cover higher loads. Priority inversion happens when a high-priority task waits for a lock held by a low one, while medium tasks keep the low one from running. Priority inheritance fixes it: the lock holder temporarily borrows the waiting task's priority.`,history:`In 1973 Chung Laung Liu and James Layland published the founding paper on scheduling tasks with hard deadlines, proving the rate-monotonic bound. In 1989 John Lehoczky, Lui Sha and Ye Ding at Carnegie Mellon gave an exact test for any processor load. In September 1990 Sha, Ragunathan Rajkumar and Lehoczky published the priority inheritance and priority ceiling protocols. In 1993 the Software Engineering Institute's handbook taught rate-monotonic analysis to working engineers, and POSIX added real-time extensions to Unix. In 1995 the real-time annex of Ada 95 built priority ceilings into the language. In July 1997 the Mars Pathfinder lander kept resetting itself because of priority inversion, until engineers on Earth switched on priority inheritance by remote command.`,facts:[`The rate-monotonic bound falls towards ln 2, about 69.3% processor use (1973).`,`Pathfinder landed on Mars on 4 July 1997.`]},"tech:osek":{intro:`OSEK/VDX is a standard small operating system for the control units in cars, such as engine, brake and door controllers. By agreeing on one interface for tasks, events and messages, carmakers and their suppliers can move software between different chips and different suppliers instead of rewriting it for each in-house system.`,how:`Everything is fixed before the car is built: a configuration file written in the OSEK Implementation Language lists every task, its priority, the events it waits for, its shared resources and its alarms, and a generator produces a kernel containing exactly those. There is no dynamic memory and no loading of programs, so the system fits in a few kilobytes and its timing can be proven. Shared resources use the priority ceiling protocol, so unbounded priority inversion and deadlock cannot happen.`,history:`In 1993 BMW, Bosch, Daimler-Benz, Opel, Siemens, Volkswagen and the University of Karlsruhe founded OSEK, short for a German name meaning open systems and their interfaces for the electronics in motor vehicles. In 1994 the French carmakers PSA and Renault joined with their own VDX project, the Vehicle Distributed eXecutive. By 1995 the merged group had issued a joint operating system specification, with matching standards for communication and network management. In 2005 the standard was published internationally as ISO 17356. From 2005 the AUTOSAR partnership built its own operating system on top of OSEK's.`,facts:[`OSEK OS defined four conformance classes, from BCC1 to ECC2 (1995).`,`ISO 17356 made OSEK an international standard in 2005.`]},"tech:misra":{intro:`MISRA C is a rulebook for writing C safely in cars and other machines where a software fault can injure people. C lets programmers do almost anything, including things whose result the language leaves undefined; MISRA forbids the features and habits that cause most such bugs, so code is predictable and easier to check.`,how:`Each rule is either required or advisory, and every exception must be justified in writing. Typical rules forbid dynamic memory allocation, which can fail unpredictably, recursion, which can overflow the stack, and goto; require every switch statement to have a default case; and ban mixing signed and unsigned numbers without an explicit conversion. Static analysis tools check most rules automatically, so a whole code base can be measured for compliance before release.`,history:`In 1994 the UK's Motor Industry Software Reliability Association, formed by carmakers, suppliers and universities, published guidelines for developing vehicle software. In 1998 it published 'Guidelines for the Use of the C Language in Vehicle Based Software', with 127 rules. In 2004 a revised edition grew to 141 rules and spread to aerospace, rail and medical devices. In 2005 Lockheed Martin's coding rules for the C++ software of the F-35 fighter drew heavily on MISRA. In 2012 a third edition reorganised the rules for newer versions of C. In October 2013 an expert witness in a US court case over Toyota's unintended acceleration testified that the throttle software broke MISRA rules tens of thousands of times.`,facts:[`MISRA C grew from 127 rules (1998) to 141 (2004) and 143 plus 16 directives (2012).`,`ISO 26262 recommends coding guidelines such as MISRA C for car software (2011).`]},"tech:adas":{intro:`Camera driver assistance puts a small camera behind the windscreen and a dedicated chip that recognises cars, lane markings and pedestrians in its images. It warns the driver of a coming collision or a drift out of lane, and can brake by itself when the driver does not react in time.`,how:`The camera sends video frames to a vision processor that looks for the shapes of vehicles, people and lines, using patterns learned from huge libraries of labelled road images. From how fast a car ahead grows in the picture, the system estimates the time to collision without needing a radar to measure distance. If the time drops too low it beeps, tightens the seat belts or applies the brakes through the car's network. One camera replaces several costly sensors.`,history:`In 1999 a start-up set out to do with one cheap camera what others did with radar and lasers. In 2008 its first vision chip, built with STMicroelectronics, was going into cars from makers such as BMW, General Motors and Volvo. In 2008 Subaru's EyeSight used a pair of cameras to brake automatically on cars sold in Japan. In October 2014 Tesla launched Autopilot using the start-up's chip. In March 2017 Intel agreed to buy the company for about $15.3 billion.`,facts:[`The leading maker's vision chips were in 160 car models from 18 makers (2014).`,`Euro NCAP began testing automatic emergency braking in 2014.`]},"tech:autosar":{intro:`AUTOSAR is a common software architecture for car control units, agreed by carmakers and suppliers. It splits each unit's software into standard layers with standard interfaces, like a LEGO baseplate, so application functions from one company can run on hardware and basic software from another.`,how:`At the top sit software components, such as a window lifter's logic, which talk only to a runtime environment: a generated layer of glue code that delivers their signals whether the partner is on the same unit or across the CAN bus. Beneath it is the basic software, with an OSEK-based operating system, communication stacks for CAN, LIN and FlexRay, memory and diagnostic services, and drivers that hide the microcontroller. Everything is described in XML files, from which tools generate and configure the code.`,history:`In July 2003 BMW, Bosch, Continental, DaimlerChrysler, Siemens VDO and Volkswagen founded the AUTOSAR partnership. By 2004 Ford, PSA Peugeot Citroën, Toyota and General Motors had joined as core partners. In 2005 the partnership issued its first release of the specifications. In December 2009 Release 4.0 added features for safety-critical and multicore systems. In March 2017 the partnership added the Adaptive Platform for powerful central computers, renaming the original the Classic Platform.`,facts:[`A premium car could carry 70 to 100 small control units (2009).`,`Release 4.0 of the Classic Platform arrived in December 2009.`]},"tech:funcsafety":{intro:`Functional safety is the discipline of proving that the electronics and software which steer, brake or accelerate a car will not hurt anyone when something goes wrong. ISO 26262 sets out how: analyse every hazard, rate its risk, design to match, and keep evidence for each step, so the maker can show why the system is safe enough.`,how:`The process starts with hazard analysis: for each function, engineers imagine failures, such as unintended braking, and rate their severity, how often drivers are exposed to them and how controllable the situation is. The ratings give each safety goal an Automotive Safety Integrity Level, from ASIL A, the mildest, to ASIL D. Higher levels demand stricter methods: redundant hardware, coding rules, more testing and independent reviews. Every requirement is traced down to code and tests, and the evidence is gathered into a safety case.`,history:`In 1998 the International Electrotechnical Commission began publishing IEC 61508, a general functional safety standard for electrical and programmable systems. Between 2009 and 2011 Toyota recalled about 9 million vehicles over unintended acceleration, putting car electronics under scrutiny. In November 2011 the first edition of ISO 26262, adapted from IEC 61508 for road cars, was published. In March 2014 Toyota agreed to pay $1.2 billion to settle a US criminal investigation into how it had handled the problems. In December 2018 the second edition extended the standard to trucks, buses and motorcycles and added guidance for semiconductors. In 2019 ISO 21448 added rules for the safety of intended functions, such as a camera system misjudging a scene without any fault.`,facts:[`ISO 26262's first edition had 10 parts; the second had 12 (2018).`,`ASIL D, the strictest level, applies to functions such as steering and braking (2011).`]},"tech:ota":{intro:`Over-the-air updates let a carmaker send new software to cars already on the road, the way a phone downloads an update. The car fetches the update over the mobile network and installs it while parked, and in the morning it may brake better, drive further on a charge or have new features, with no visit to a dealer.`,how:`A telematics unit with its own mobile connection downloads a signed update package. The car checks the signature so that only the maker's code is accepted, often installs the new version alongside the old, and switches over only when everything has checked out, keeping the old version as a fallback. Updates can reach the screens and also the controllers for the motor, battery and brakes. The maker must keep its servers, keys and software for every model secure for many years.`,history:`In 1996 GM's OnStar first linked cars to a call centre over the mobile network. In June 2012 Tesla began delivering the Model S, which received regular software updates over the air for its screens and its drive systems. In November 2013 Tesla pushed an update that raised the Model S's suspension at motorway speeds, after fires caused by road debris. In September 2015 the scandal over Volkswagen's emissions software showed the cost of the old way, as about 11 million cars needed workshop visits to fix their code. In May 2018 Tesla shortened the Model 3's braking distance with an over-the-air update days after Consumer Reports criticised it. In January 2021 a United Nations regulation, UN R156, set rules for managing car software updates.`,facts:[`Tesla's 2018 update cut the Model 3's stopping distance by about 19 feet (6 metres).`,`From July 2022 new car types in the EU needed a certified software update system.`]},"tech:adaptiveauto":{intro:`Central car computers replace many of a car's small controllers with a few powerful ones that run full operating systems and real applications. Driver assistance, infotainment and connectivity move onto these computers, which can be updated and given new features like a smartphone.`,how:`The AUTOSAR Adaptive Platform runs on a POSIX operating system, such as Linux or QNX, on multicore processors. Applications are written in C++ and talk to each other as services over the car's Ethernet network, finding each other while running instead of through fixed wiring planned years ahead. They can be installed and updated separately. The small Classic controllers remain for brakes and motors that need guaranteed timing, linked to the central computers by gateways.`,history:`In February 2009 IEEE Spectrum reported that a premium car might carry 70 to 100 small computers running about 100 million lines of code. In 2010 Research In Motion, maker of the BlackBerry, bought QNX, whose real-time system already ran many car infotainment units. In January 2015 NVIDIA showed its DRIVE PX computer for driver assistance at the Consumer Electronics Show. In 2016 Tesla began fitting its cars with an NVIDIA-based computer for Autopilot. In March 2017 AUTOSAR published release 17-03, the first of its Adaptive Platform. In 2017 the Audi A8 introduced zFAS, a central driver-assistance computer combining chips from several makers.`,facts:[`QNX ran in over 150 million vehicles in 2019 and 235 million in 2024.`,`Tesla's own self-driving computer chip replaced NVIDIA's in April 2019.`]},"tech:carandroid":{intro:`A phone operating system in the dashboard means the carmaker runs a version of a smartphone system, such as Android, directly on the car's own screens. Maps, music, a voice assistant and an app store come from the phone world and work even with no phone in the car, while the carmaker adds its own controls for climate and settings.`,how:`Android Automotive runs on the car's infotainment computer and talks to the car through a vehicle interface layer, which exposes signals such as speed, gear and temperature to approved apps under strict permissions. Carmakers can license Google's services, including Maps, Assistant and the Play Store, or use the bare open-source system with their own. Apps are adapted for driving, with large buttons and limits while the car moves. This differs from projection, where a phone merely casts its own screen to the dashboard.`,history:`In 2007 Ford launched SYNC, built on Microsoft's automotive software, for voice control of phones and music. In March 2014 Apple introduced CarPlay, which projected an iPhone's apps onto the car's screen. In May 2017 Google showed Android Automotive running cars' own screens in an Audi and a Volvo at its I/O conference. In 2018 and 2019 the Renault-Nissan-Mitsubishi alliance and General Motors chose it for future models. In July 2020 the Polestar 2 became the first production car with Android Automotive and Google's services built in. In 2023 GM said it would drop phone projection from its new electric cars in favour of its built-in system.`,facts:[`Android Auto, Google's phone projection, reached its first production cars in 2015.`,`Volvo expected to ship Android-based cars about two years after the 2017 demonstration.`]},"tech:sdv":{intro:`A software-defined vehicle is a car designed around a few central computers and their software, instead of around dozens of separate boxes from separate suppliers. Wires are grouped by where things are in the car, not by what they do, and new features, fixes and even paid upgrades arrive as software over the air.`,how:`In a zonal design a handful of zone controllers each serve one area of the car, such as the front left, powering and connecting every light, motor and sensor nearby. They link over a fast Ethernet backbone to one or two central computers that run most functions as software. Fewer, shorter wires save weight and cost and simplify assembly. Because functions live in central software, the maker can change how the car behaves for its whole life.`,history:`In July 2017 Tesla's Model 3 arrived with a strikingly centralised design, controlling most functions from a few computers. In 2020 Volkswagen's ID.3 reached buyers months late after problems with its new software. In 2023 Volkswagen's software unit, CARIAD, lost about 2.4 billion euros. In June 2024 Volkswagen agreed to invest up to $5 billion in a joint venture with Rivian to use Rivian's zonal electronics and software. In 2024 Rivian's second-generation R1 moved to a zonal design with far fewer control units.`,facts:[`Rivian's second-generation R1 cut its control units from 17 to 7 and its wiring by 1.6 miles (2024).`,`BMW's offer of heated seats by subscription in some countries drew a backlash (2022).`]}};export{e as PEDIA_PARTS};